114
00:04:39,450 --> 00:04:40,860
OK.

115
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:44,160
So with that said, let me jump
into my conceptual framework

116
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:47,700
for thinking about
multimodal model madness.

117
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:51,940
I think a unifying goal for much
of AI, I won't say all of AI,

118
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:54,640
but much of it is to
build world models.

119
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:58,020
And by that, I
mean a system that

120
00:04:58,020 --> 00:05:00,400
can simulate the future
state of the world,

121
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:03,610
conditioned on observations of
the current state of the world,

122
00:05:03,610 --> 00:05:05,940
and also being able
to simulate forward

123
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:09,670
the effects that system's
actions will have on the future.

124
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:13,440
So this necessarily means
you have the reality that

125
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:16,050
is out there, you have sensors
that collect information

126
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:19,090
about that reality, you perceive
it with your world model,

127
00:05:19,090 --> 00:05:21,540
and ideally, you're able
to run a simulation of,

128
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,970
OK, well, if I take this
action, how will the world

129
00:05:23,970 --> 00:05:25,480
change in the future?

130
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:28,033
And that is a very powerful
and generic framework

131
00:05:28,033 --> 00:05:30,450
for being able to reason about
why the world is the way it

132
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:32,320
is, how you should act.

133
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:35,520
It obviously plays into
planning and perception.

134
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:38,400
So one thing that
I think is obvious

135
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,990
is that the world is
perceived, at least by us,

136
00:05:40,990 --> 00:05:44,200
and the world models in our
heads in a multimodal way.

137
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:49,660
So you have visual modality
that will tell you, for example,

138
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:52,530
that this roundabout that
I biked through many times

139
00:05:52,530 --> 00:05:55,080
on my way to lab is very
dangerous and chaotic when

140
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:57,750
people are running late
to class and panicking.

141
00:05:57,750 --> 00:06:00,310
But you're not just getting
the visual information,

142
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:02,860
you, of course, also
have the audio modality,

143
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:05,370
which comes from a different
sensor, a microphone

144
00:06:05,370 --> 00:06:09,360
for a machine or ears for us,
and you get an audio stream.

145
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:12,910
And often when you encounter
video or image data,

146
00:06:12,910 --> 00:06:14,940
you could also have a
text representation.

147
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:16,440
Someone might have
written a caption

148
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:19,140
under their video saying,
whoa, this roundabout

149
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:22,350
is extremely chaotic and I'm
going to try to avoid it.

150
00:06:22,350 --> 00:06:26,682
My thesis, or my assumption
is that the best world models

151
00:06:26,682 --> 00:06:28,140
are going to be
able to incorporate

152
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:30,720
all of these modalities
and make decisions

153
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:33,810
about what to do based, not just
on one of them, but all of them

154
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:35,080
in a productive way.

155
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:38,220
So when you're running the world
model in your head, where you're

156
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:41,530
perceiving the data from
all these modalities,

157
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:43,320
you might have a
question like, what

158
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:46,900
will happen if I start walking
forward into this roundabout?

159
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:49,590
And because you
have the combination

160
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:52,090
of maybe someone warned
you ahead of time,

161
00:06:52,090 --> 00:06:54,370
you can hear what's going
on, can see what's going on,

162
00:06:54,370 --> 00:06:56,412
you're able to predict
that you might get crashed

163
00:06:56,412 --> 00:07:00,720
into by-- somebody on a bike
who's running late for class.

164
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:04,630
The task of that model is
effectively multimodal learning.

165
00:07:04,630 --> 00:07:06,030
You need to know
how to integrate

166
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:08,880
these various information
streams, which natively come

167
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:10,492
in many different
formats, and you

168
00:07:10,492 --> 00:07:12,450
need to learn from them
in a way that gives you

169
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:15,300
the best chance of making
accurate simulations of what's

170
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:18,570
going to happen in the world.

171
00:07:18,570 --> 00:07:21,640
I want to make a distinction
that I think is important

172
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:23,640
and will come up a couple
of times in this talk,

173
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:26,190
about two reasons you
might care about combining

174
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:27,550
multimodal streams.

175
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:30,540
One of them is the idea that
you could do multimodality

176
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:31,553
as translation.

177
00:07:31,553 --> 00:07:32,970
You could say,
there's information

178
00:07:32,970 --> 00:07:36,160
in the visual domain, there's
information in the audio domain,

179
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:38,850
and what I want to do is have
a representation of the world

180
00:07:38,850 --> 00:07:41,700
that merges those
things so that I've

181
00:07:41,700 --> 00:07:43,650
captured all of the
information about one

182
00:07:43,650 --> 00:07:46,680
modality in the other modality.

183
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:50,163
The other thing that you might
want to do is disambiguation.

184
00:07:50,163 --> 00:07:52,080
So there could be
information that just is not

185
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:53,560
available in one modality.

186
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:57,000
And So you have a blind spot,
both figuratively and literally

187
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,380
here, where if you
only had a camera,

188
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:01,810
you wouldn't know everything
about the state of the world,

189
00:08:01,810 --> 00:08:05,580
and you're going to need to
incorporate this other modality

190
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:07,500
to get a full picture
of what is happening

191
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:08,710
in the world around you.

192
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:11,608
So I'll try to talk a little
bit as I'm giving examples

193
00:08:11,608 --> 00:08:13,650
in the literature and in
our work about whether I

194
00:08:13,650 --> 00:08:17,080
think we're doing translation
or disambiguation.

195
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:19,800

196
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:24,040
So translation has a
very familiar shape.

197
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:27,330
It's about capturing all
of the information in one

198
00:08:27,330 --> 00:08:29,290
modality and another modality.

199
00:08:29,290 --> 00:08:31,270
So when you're
building, for example,

200
00:08:31,270 --> 00:08:34,590
a text to image
system, what you want

201
00:08:34,590 --> 00:08:36,450
is for all of the
information in the prompt

202
00:08:36,450 --> 00:08:37,950
that you're sending
to the system

203
00:08:37,950 --> 00:08:39,970
to be reflected in the image.

204
00:08:39,970 --> 00:08:43,350
You want to basically
push these things together

205
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:46,670
so that when I include a bit of
information in the text stream,

206
00:08:46,670 --> 00:08:51,100
I want it reflected back
to me in the image stream.

207
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:53,560
And when I talk about
disambiguation, what I mean

208
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:55,270
is that you need
to piece together

209
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:58,330
information that is only
going to be available in one

210
00:08:58,330 --> 00:08:59,540
modality or another.

211
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:02,230
So for example, let's say
you're approaching this building

212
00:09:02,230 --> 00:09:04,270
and you see everyone
sprinting out of it.

213
00:09:04,270 --> 00:09:05,695
Now, there's some
ambiguity here.

214
00:09:05,695 --> 00:09:08,320
There are actually many reasons
why people could be running out

215
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:09,520
of the building.

216
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:12,070
Now, if you combine that
with your sense of hearing,

217
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:15,520
you might hear that there is
a fire alarm going off inside,

218
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:17,812
and that rapidly disambiguates
which state of the world

219
00:09:17,812 --> 00:09:20,228
you're in, that there's a fire
and everyone's running away

220
00:09:20,228 --> 00:09:20,840
from it.

221
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:23,398
But you could also have
a second scenario where

222
00:09:23,398 --> 00:09:24,940
you hear an announcement
that there's

223
00:09:24,940 --> 00:09:27,740
free boba for the first 10
students that get to it,

224
00:09:27,740 --> 00:09:29,960
and now everyone is sprinting
out of the building,

225
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:31,720
and your world
model should update.

226
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:33,998
If you want boba, you
need to turn around and go

227
00:09:33,998 --> 00:09:36,040
one direction, maybe to
where the announcement is

228
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:36,920
saying to go.

229
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:39,225
If there's a fire, you head
to the nearest evacuation

230
00:09:39,225 --> 00:09:40,600
point or whatever
it is we're all

231
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:41,892
supposed to do if that happens.

232
00:09:41,892 --> 00:09:44,740

233
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:50,110
OK, the next few slides will be
a tour that I will acknowledge

234
00:09:50,110 --> 00:09:52,330
is both brief and
incomplete, about how

235
00:09:52,330 --> 00:09:56,650
people have tried to
incorporate multimodal streams

236
00:09:56,650 --> 00:09:58,040
in the literature.

237
00:09:58,040 --> 00:10:01,160
And I'm really going to
go over five things here.

238
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:02,660
You don't have to
memorize them now.

239
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:04,160
I'll go through them one by one.

240
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:05,770
But one thing that
I want to call out

241
00:10:05,770 --> 00:10:10,390
is, when people talk about
multimodality or merging streams

242
00:10:10,390 --> 00:10:13,690
from one modality
and another, there's

243
00:10:13,690 --> 00:10:15,820
often this nomenclature
of whether you're doing

244
00:10:15,820 --> 00:10:17,810
early fusion or late fusion.

245
00:10:17,810 --> 00:10:19,510
And what people
usually mean by that

246
00:10:19,510 --> 00:10:21,760
is, are you bringing
the modalities together

247
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:25,270
as soon as possible and then
running your processing forward

248
00:10:25,270 --> 00:10:27,880
from there, or do you
process each information

249
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:30,470
stream separately and then bring
them together at the very end

250
00:10:30,470 --> 00:10:32,440
when you actually need
to make a decision

251
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:34,850
about the state of the world
and your actions in it?

252
00:10:34,850 --> 00:10:39,100
So I have to admit that
this framework doesn't

253
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:41,030
help me think about
this space too much,

254
00:10:41,030 --> 00:10:43,930
but I've tried to map
onto these five things

255
00:10:43,930 --> 00:10:45,500
where I think they fall.

256
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:48,485
And there's a range here
from super early fusion

257
00:10:48,485 --> 00:10:50,110
to what I would think
of as late fusion

258
00:10:50,110 --> 00:10:51,560
and everything in between.

259
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:54,790
So we'll see if you agree
with me as I go through it.

260
00:10:54,790 --> 00:10:56,560
The first thing I'm
going to talk about

261
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:58,790
is learning joint
embedding spaces.

262
00:10:58,790 --> 00:11:01,240
So a model that many of
you might be familiar with

263
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:04,330
is the CLIP approach for
combining the modalities

264
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:06,370
of image and text.

265
00:11:06,370 --> 00:11:09,790
The primary idea here is to
do contrastive learning, where

266
00:11:09,790 --> 00:11:13,240
you have two encoders,
an image encoder.

267
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:15,370
Let's see if my mouse shows up.

268
00:11:15,370 --> 00:11:17,920
You have an image encoder
that pushes images down

269
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:20,500
into an embedding, and
you have a text encoder

270
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:23,140
that takes
corresponding captions

271
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:25,640
and pushes those down
into an embedding.

272
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:28,240
And the objective
for the model is

273
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:31,480
to make the embeddings for
corresponding image and text

274
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:34,060
pairs as similar as
possible, while pushing away

275
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:36,440
the representations
of mismatched pairs.

276
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:39,760
For example, the similarity
between the embedding

277
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,010
for image 2 and caption 1
should be as low as possible,

278
00:11:43,010 --> 00:11:45,880
while increasing the similarity
between the embedding for image

279
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:47,060
1 and text 1.

280
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:50,740
And this is explicitly
trying to learn

281
00:11:50,740 --> 00:11:53,650
a single joint
representation space where

282
00:11:53,650 --> 00:11:55,373
if you pick a point
in that space,

283
00:11:55,373 --> 00:11:58,040
there should be kind of an image
that corresponds to that point,

284
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:01,520
and also a text sample that
also corresponds to that point.

285
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:05,170
And that way you can translate
between the text modality

286
00:12:05,170 --> 00:12:06,710
and the image modality.

287
00:12:06,710 --> 00:12:09,520
You can see this
explicitly in the paper

288
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:11,300
on the right of this figure.

289
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:13,820
You can see that what they're
doing is taking an image,

290
00:12:13,820 --> 00:12:15,700
figuring out what the
embedding of that image

291
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:18,010
is with the image encoder,
and then searching

292
00:12:18,010 --> 00:12:21,970
over many possible text
inputs that will bring you

293
00:12:21,970 --> 00:12:24,430
as close as possible to that
point in the representation

294
00:12:24,430 --> 00:12:25,330
space.

295
00:12:25,330 --> 00:12:28,850
So I would consider this
actually relatively late fusion,

296
00:12:28,850 --> 00:12:31,960
because you're learning the
image encoder pretty much

297
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:33,500
separately from
the text encoder.

298
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:36,010
Those don't interact until
there's pressure through

299
00:12:36,010 --> 00:12:38,680
the loss function to
make those embeddings as

300
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:39,540
similar as possible.

301
00:12:39,540 --> 00:12:43,420

302
00:12:43,420 --> 00:12:45,638
I would say, I don't want
to call it the grown up

303
00:12:45,638 --> 00:12:47,680
version of this, but an
expanded version of this,

304
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:49,520
is the image bind approach.

305
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:51,440
And if people aren't
familiar with this,

306
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:53,560
it is still a contrastive
learning objective

307
00:12:53,560 --> 00:12:54,860
between modalities.

308
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:57,680
But instead of just doing
images and captions,

309
00:12:57,680 --> 00:12:59,800
it uses images as
kind of an anchor

310
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:04,300
and learns pairwise contrastive
between images and text, images

311
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:06,650
and depth maps,
images and audio.

312
00:13:06,650 --> 00:13:08,500
And the underlying
objective here

313
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:10,750
is the same, that you want
a single representation

314
00:13:10,750 --> 00:13:13,000
space where it doesn't
matter which modality you

315
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:13,910
came in through.

316
00:13:13,910 --> 00:13:15,970
Once you know where you
are, you can back out

317
00:13:15,970 --> 00:13:18,170
what all the modalities
should look like.

318
00:13:18,170 --> 00:13:20,200
And in fact, in
this paper, they do

319
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:23,680
some interesting
demonstrations of that space

320
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:26,410
by doing things like
embedding space arithmetic,

321
00:13:26,410 --> 00:13:28,960
or prompting with one
modality and seeing

322
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:31,326
where you fall in that
space on other modalities.

323
00:13:31,326 --> 00:13:33,850

324
00:13:33,850 --> 00:13:37,460
The other option that I
have done occasionally,

325
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:40,010
but I think is not popular
for technical reasons,

326
00:13:40,010 --> 00:13:42,010
is super, super,
super early fusion

327
00:13:42,010 --> 00:13:45,140
where you can just directly
concatenate raw inputs.

328
00:13:45,140 --> 00:13:47,180
So consider if you
had, for example,

329
00:13:47,180 --> 00:13:51,895
a 3D RGB color image and
you also had a depth image.

330
00:13:51,895 --> 00:13:53,770
What you could do is
just staple on the depth

331
00:13:53,770 --> 00:13:56,812
image as a fourth channel,
and then train a ConvNet,

332
00:13:56,812 --> 00:13:59,020
or train your vision
transformer and pretend that you

333
00:13:59,020 --> 00:14:01,300
had four channels all along.

334
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:04,300
That is a totally valid way
to incorporate information

335
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:05,990
from multiple modalities.

336
00:14:05,990 --> 00:14:08,560
The reason that I think
it is not super popular

337
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:11,630
is it requires that there's
something to concatenate on.

338
00:14:11,630 --> 00:14:14,950
So people typically will
first project raw inputs down

339
00:14:14,950 --> 00:14:17,620
to tokens and then concatenate
token streams, which

340
00:14:17,620 --> 00:14:18,920
I'll talk about in a second.

341
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:21,320
But if you want to
jump really, really,

342
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:23,720
really to the front of
that encoding stack,

343
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:26,920
sometimes you have your height
and width dimensions on images

344
00:14:26,920 --> 00:14:30,970
line up and you can just
concatenate directly.

345
00:14:30,970 --> 00:14:34,730
The third option that you
could do is cross-attention.

346
00:14:34,730 --> 00:14:37,930
So now, we're getting much
more transformer-specific.

347
00:14:37,930 --> 00:14:41,560
The idea here is you have
two token streams, one

348
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:43,000
from each modality.

349
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,020
In the example
from the Lu et al.

350
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:48,637
2019 paper, you have one token
stream that comes from images,

351
00:14:48,637 --> 00:14:50,470
and then you also have
a second token stream

352
00:14:50,470 --> 00:14:51,680
that comes from text.

353
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:53,360
So those are your
two modalities.

354
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:55,630
You can have as many
self-attention layers

355
00:14:55,630 --> 00:14:57,320
as you want within each stream.

356
00:14:57,320 --> 00:15:00,440
But then you can also have
these cross-attention layers,

357
00:15:00,440 --> 00:15:05,110
which are shown here in the
diagram, where you're basically

358
00:15:05,110 --> 00:15:08,840
giving each modality the
chance to be the primary.

359
00:15:08,840 --> 00:15:11,170
The primary will
generate the keys--

360
00:15:11,170 --> 00:15:13,490
or sorry, the queries in
the attention operation,

361
00:15:13,490 --> 00:15:16,400
and the secondary stream will
provide the keys and values.

362
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:19,430
And because that is an
asymmetric procedure,

363
00:15:19,430 --> 00:15:22,930
you will often see these
cross-attention layers

364
00:15:22,930 --> 00:15:25,880
duplicated on either side
of the processing stream,

365
00:15:25,880 --> 00:15:28,420
so that, in one
example, the images

366
00:15:28,420 --> 00:15:30,820
get to provide the queries
and then the other side of it

367
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:35,150
the text stream gets
to provide the queries.

368
00:15:35,150 --> 00:15:37,940
I broke this out just because
when I was first implementing

369
00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:39,480
this, it was helpful
to think about,

370
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:42,170
but if you look at what
is actually happening

371
00:15:42,170 --> 00:15:45,740
in an attention layer,
you have your input x,

372
00:15:45,740 --> 00:15:48,860
you will have a linear
projection to your queries

373
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:52,070
Q, your keys K,
and your values V.

374
00:15:52,070 --> 00:15:55,730
And then those go into your
scaled dot product attention

375
00:15:55,730 --> 00:15:59,580
operator and then linear
projection and dropout.

376
00:15:59,580 --> 00:16:01,640
All you're doing in a
cross-attention layer

377
00:16:01,640 --> 00:16:04,590
is you're taking input
from another stream y,

378
00:16:04,590 --> 00:16:07,080
and you're using that to
generate the keys and values,

379
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:09,300
but everything else stays as is.

380
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:11,900
This gives you a chance to
have the two information

381
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:14,220
streams mingle somewhere in
the middle of the network.

382
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:16,340
So I would call this
intermediate fusion,

383
00:16:16,340 --> 00:16:20,300
depending which layers
you're doing it in.

384
00:16:20,300 --> 00:16:21,980
The fourth option,
which I alluded

385
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:24,470
to when I was talking
about concatenation,

386
00:16:24,470 --> 00:16:27,330
is you can just slap
tokens onto a stream.

387
00:16:27,330 --> 00:16:31,610
I think one thing that has
really resonated with me as we

388
00:16:31,610 --> 00:16:34,107
move into using transformers
in more and more domains,

389
00:16:34,107 --> 00:16:36,440
the fact that everything is
just tokens all the way down

390
00:16:36,440 --> 00:16:39,600
makes it really easy to add
more tokens to a sequence,

391
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:42,830
and they can come from other
modalities if you'd like.

392
00:16:42,830 --> 00:16:44,610
One way that this
happens all the time,

393
00:16:44,610 --> 00:16:45,985
but I think we
don't really think

394
00:16:45,985 --> 00:16:48,140
of it as multimodal
learning, is if you

395
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:52,760
have, for example, a vision
transformer, you might add on.

396
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:55,610
In this case, you're
prepending a CLS token

397
00:16:55,610 --> 00:16:57,500
that really is like--

398
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:59,310
you could think of
it as text, I guess,

399
00:16:59,310 --> 00:17:02,120
because the class is
literally a text label.

400
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:04,230
But you have an
embedding for the text,

401
00:17:04,230 --> 00:17:05,780
and that just goes
and participates

402
00:17:05,780 --> 00:17:08,780
in the stream of
tokens and participates

403
00:17:08,780 --> 00:17:10,410
in the self-attention
operations.

404
00:17:10,410 --> 00:17:11,160
And there you go.

405
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:14,424
You've integrated a text
label with your image data.

406
00:17:14,424 --> 00:17:17,162

407
00:17:17,162 --> 00:17:18,829
The other place you
see this is actually

408
00:17:18,829 --> 00:17:21,119
in the DALL-E 1 implementation.

409
00:17:21,119 --> 00:17:24,829
So the way that this worked
is you have text tokens, which

410
00:17:24,829 --> 00:17:26,780
are captions for images.

411
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:28,820
You also have image
tokens, which in this case

412
00:17:28,820 --> 00:17:31,880
come from learning a
codebook with a discrete VAE.

413
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:35,760
And you concatenate on the image
tokens after the text tokens.

414
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:38,780
And then at inference time, you
can provide only the text tokens

415
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:42,000
and autoregressively predict
what the image tokens should be.

416
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:45,020
So again, you've solved
the multimodality problem

417
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:47,730
by first boiling everything
down into token space.

418
00:17:47,730 --> 00:17:50,150
And then, once they're
tokens, your decoder

419
00:17:50,150 --> 00:17:51,930
doesn't need to know
which ones are image

420
00:17:51,930 --> 00:17:52,930
and which ones are text.

421
00:17:52,930 --> 00:17:55,950

422
00:17:55,950 --> 00:17:57,950
And finally, something
that we have increasingly

423
00:17:57,950 --> 00:18:01,710
been using at Noetik is this
adaptive layer norm idea.

424
00:18:01,710 --> 00:18:03,300
So I really like this figure.

425
00:18:03,300 --> 00:18:05,960
This is from the diffusion
transformer paper.

426
00:18:05,960 --> 00:18:07,805
What I like about
it is that they also

427
00:18:07,805 --> 00:18:09,930
talk about two other things
I've already mentioned.

428
00:18:09,930 --> 00:18:12,560
The middle column there
shows how you might

429
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:13,957
do this with cross-attention.

430
00:18:13,957 --> 00:18:16,040
I guess I should back up
and say, in the diffusion

431
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:19,610
transformer case, the extra
modality you're feeding in

432
00:18:19,610 --> 00:18:21,810
is a conditioning token.

433
00:18:21,810 --> 00:18:25,080
You want to guide the diffusion
in a particular direction.

434
00:18:25,080 --> 00:18:27,950
So you have some
text label and you

435
00:18:27,950 --> 00:18:31,550
want that to interact
with the main transformer

436
00:18:31,550 --> 00:18:34,910
block that is going to
be doing the diffusion.

437
00:18:34,910 --> 00:18:36,830
So you could do that
with cross-attention,

438
00:18:36,830 --> 00:18:37,862
which is shown here.

439
00:18:37,862 --> 00:18:39,320
You could do it
with what is called

440
00:18:39,320 --> 00:18:42,260
in this paper, in-context
conditioning, which is just

441
00:18:42,260 --> 00:18:43,560
the concatenation approach.

442
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:44,990
You can actually see that here.

443
00:18:44,990 --> 00:18:48,445
Concatenate on the sequence
dimension, as we just saw.

444
00:18:48,445 --> 00:18:50,070
But the thing introduced
in this paper,

445
00:18:50,070 --> 00:18:52,220
which is very parameter
efficient and cool that it

446
00:18:52,220 --> 00:18:56,960
works, is you can take that
conditioning information

447
00:18:56,960 --> 00:19:00,380
and use effectively a
linear layer to push it

448
00:19:00,380 --> 00:19:02,850
into small scalar parameters.

449
00:19:02,850 --> 00:19:05,420
So here, what they're doing
is predicting an alpha, beta

450
00:19:05,420 --> 00:19:06,300
and gamma.

451
00:19:06,300 --> 00:19:07,940
They actually
predict two triplets

452
00:19:07,940 --> 00:19:11,390
of alpha, beta, gamma and
use those in the layer norm

453
00:19:11,390 --> 00:19:12,360
operation.

454
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:14,870
So this is just
another way, as far

455
00:19:14,870 --> 00:19:17,810
as I understand, to push
information from one modality

456
00:19:17,810 --> 00:19:20,390
and have it steer or
influence or guide

457
00:19:20,390 --> 00:19:22,220
what is happening
in the processing

458
00:19:22,220 --> 00:19:25,850
of the primary sensory stream.

459
00:19:25,850 --> 00:19:27,330
I have a little
code snippet here.

460
00:19:27,330 --> 00:19:29,622
Probably not worth lingering
on, but it's just striking

461
00:19:29,622 --> 00:19:31,850
how straightforward
this approach is.

462
00:19:31,850 --> 00:19:34,970
Really, all that's happening is
you have a single linear layer

463
00:19:34,970 --> 00:19:37,350
in here hiding after
a non-linearity,

464
00:19:37,350 --> 00:19:39,200
and then you're using
that to determine

465
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:43,115
the parameters for shifting and
scaling operations in LayerNorm.

466
00:19:43,115 --> 00:19:44,990
And this turns out to
be a very effective way

467
00:19:44,990 --> 00:19:48,590
to get information about a
text-based conditioning label

468
00:19:48,590 --> 00:19:52,190
into the image domain.

469
00:19:52,190 --> 00:19:55,760
OK, this is a pretty good
place to pause and see

470
00:19:55,760 --> 00:20:00,710
if anyone has any
questions before I move on.

471
00:20:00,710 --> 00:20:05,090
Yeah, particularly when we
talk about the cross-attention,

472
00:20:05,090 --> 00:20:08,480
can cross-attention
on LayerNorm, 1,

473
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:16,820
could be used as the other
LayerNorm as a linear reference?

474
00:20:16,820 --> 00:20:19,940
Yeah, if I understand correctly.

475
00:20:19,940 --> 00:20:22,500
I think you can.

476
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:25,490
And the thing that you
run into here, that's

477
00:20:25,490 --> 00:20:26,770
both a blessing and a curse.

478
00:20:26,770 --> 00:20:28,630
It's like you can do
pretty much anything.

479
00:20:28,630 --> 00:20:32,220
At some point, if you think that
there's more useful processing

480
00:20:32,220 --> 00:20:35,790
to do before you derive these
LayerNorm shift and scale

481
00:20:35,790 --> 00:20:38,320
parameters, this doesn't
have to be a linear layer.

482
00:20:38,320 --> 00:20:40,690
It can be an arbitrarily
complicated function.

483
00:20:40,690 --> 00:20:42,690
It could be a
cross-attention layer

484
00:20:42,690 --> 00:20:47,260
that is using the information
from two streams that way,

485
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:49,120
and then also doing a LayerNorm.

486
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:51,550
The question is, is it
parameter efficient,

487
00:20:51,550 --> 00:20:53,820
does it work
empirically, and do you

488
00:20:53,820 --> 00:20:55,510
have a good principled
reason to do it?

489
00:20:55,510 --> 00:20:56,940
And I think often
that third thing

490
00:20:56,940 --> 00:20:59,440
is missing in a lot
of these approaches,

491
00:20:59,440 --> 00:21:01,240
which is just like,
yeah, it seems to work.

492
00:21:01,240 --> 00:21:03,330
so we're going to roll with it.

493
00:21:03,330 --> 00:21:06,300
Thank you.

494
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:07,540
Yeah.

495
00:21:07,540 --> 00:21:10,230
Can you speak to
that complex level.

496
00:21:10,230 --> 00:21:12,360
I mean, maybe on
a conceptual level

497
00:21:12,360 --> 00:21:15,580
on why in a cancer
research space,

498
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:18,700
that transformer is important in
the sense that does it combine,

499
00:21:18,700 --> 00:21:23,130
let's say an image of a
patient or let's say biopsy

500
00:21:23,130 --> 00:21:25,930
and maybe the text
description of this.

501
00:21:25,930 --> 00:21:31,330
So what makes transformers so
relevant for cancer research

502
00:21:31,330 --> 00:21:32,200
in a bigger context?

503
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:32,700
Yeah.

504
00:21:32,700 --> 00:21:34,950
So the question is,
why are transformers

505
00:21:34,950 --> 00:21:36,550
important for cancer research?

506
00:21:36,550 --> 00:21:40,930
And I think the answers could
have basically, two shapes.

507
00:21:40,930 --> 00:21:43,770
One of them is if you format
your data and your task

508
00:21:43,770 --> 00:21:48,030
properly, I think transformers
are both simpler to scale

509
00:21:48,030 --> 00:21:51,820
and empirically scale better
than other approaches.

510
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:53,370
The second reason,
I would say is not

511
00:21:53,370 --> 00:21:56,470
all data modalities are
amenable to other architectures.

512
00:21:56,470 --> 00:21:58,240
So sometimes you
do have image data,

513
00:21:58,240 --> 00:22:00,100
and I'll show examples
of that in a second.

514
00:22:00,100 --> 00:22:02,453
Sometimes you have data that
looks a lot more like text,

515
00:22:02,453 --> 00:22:04,620
and so you want to use the
approaches that have been

516
00:22:04,620 --> 00:22:06,570
successful in modeling text.

517
00:22:06,570 --> 00:22:08,070
And then the third
thing I would say

518
00:22:08,070 --> 00:22:11,080
is that specifically
for multimodal learning,

519
00:22:11,080 --> 00:22:13,650
it's extremely helpful to
be able to push everything

520
00:22:13,650 --> 00:22:16,820
into token soup and bring it
back together however you want.

521
00:22:16,820 --> 00:22:18,570
So it turns out that
transformers are just

522
00:22:18,570 --> 00:22:20,610
a very good substrate
for combining

523
00:22:20,610 --> 00:22:22,530
kind of arbitrarily
creative ideas

524
00:22:22,530 --> 00:22:25,020
about how to integrate
various measurements,

525
00:22:25,020 --> 00:22:26,520
but in a couple of
slides, I'll show

526
00:22:26,520 --> 00:22:28,020
exactly what those
measurements are,

527
00:22:28,020 --> 00:22:30,000
and I'll walk through
architectures we're using

528
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:31,550
to learn from patient data.

529
00:22:31,550 --> 00:22:34,330

530
00:22:34,330 --> 00:22:34,830
Cool?

531
00:22:34,830 --> 00:22:37,890
OK, I will go ahead and do that.

532
00:22:37,890 --> 00:22:42,270
So as a reminder, this is
kind of my mental model

533
00:22:42,270 --> 00:22:44,560
for how things work in
the macroscopic world.

534
00:22:44,560 --> 00:22:47,820
And I think the analogy
in the microscopic world

535
00:22:47,820 --> 00:22:51,010
is, now we want to understand
the world of cancer biology

536
00:22:51,010 --> 00:22:54,990
and the queries that we want
to pose to this world model

537
00:22:54,990 --> 00:22:57,880
are not things like what happens
if I walk into a roundabout?

538
00:22:57,880 --> 00:23:00,810
They are things like,
what will happen if I

539
00:23:00,810 --> 00:23:02,320
give this drug to this patient?

540
00:23:02,320 --> 00:23:04,210
Will the tumor go away or not?

541
00:23:04,210 --> 00:23:06,150
And you want--
effectively a simulator

542
00:23:06,150 --> 00:23:08,050
that can help you
answer those questions.

543
00:23:08,050 --> 00:23:09,540
I will fill in
the question marks

544
00:23:09,540 --> 00:23:11,460
momentarily about
what these sensors are

545
00:23:11,460 --> 00:23:12,820
and what the data looks like.

546
00:23:12,820 --> 00:23:15,990
But I want to put this
up front as our goal is

547
00:23:15,990 --> 00:23:17,730
to take this world
modeling approach

548
00:23:17,730 --> 00:23:20,670
and to build a system that we
can use to simulate answers

549
00:23:20,670 --> 00:23:23,940
to clinically
relevant questions.

550
00:23:23,940 --> 00:23:27,280
OK, this is my crash course
in cancer immunology.

551
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:30,000
I was talking to a card-carrying
immunologist this morning

552
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:33,450
and apologized for trying to
boil down their entire career

553
00:23:33,450 --> 00:23:34,720
into a few bullet points.

554
00:23:34,720 --> 00:23:38,710
So please know that this
is a gross simplification.

555
00:23:38,710 --> 00:23:41,880
The basic idea behind
cancer immunotherapy

556
00:23:41,880 --> 00:23:44,940
is that we know that in
some cases, [? deer ?]

557
00:23:44,940 --> 00:23:48,040
immune system can detect
and destroy cancers.

558
00:23:48,040 --> 00:23:50,940
But tumors are sneaky
and they evolve

559
00:23:50,940 --> 00:23:52,950
to either hide from
the immune system

560
00:23:52,950 --> 00:23:55,620
or to actively suppress
the immune system so

561
00:23:55,620 --> 00:24:00,240
that the immune system can't do
its job and destroy the tumor.

562
00:24:00,240 --> 00:24:03,060
The idea behind immunotherapy
as a type of treatment

563
00:24:03,060 --> 00:24:06,630
is you want to basically get
rid of those immune evasion

564
00:24:06,630 --> 00:24:09,310
mechanisms or the immune
suppression mechanisms,

565
00:24:09,310 --> 00:24:12,340
and help reactivate immune
cells to help them do their job.

566
00:24:12,340 --> 00:24:14,700
So you already have the
machinery in your body

567
00:24:14,700 --> 00:24:17,482
to fight cancer, it's
just losing that fight,

568
00:24:17,482 --> 00:24:19,440
and you want to find a
way to tip the scales so

569
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:22,770
that the immune system
can start winning.

570
00:24:22,770 --> 00:24:24,840
The way that we do
that could have two

571
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:27,540
shapes, and we're interested
in both of them at Noetik.

572
00:24:27,540 --> 00:24:29,820
One of them is you
just find new drugs

573
00:24:29,820 --> 00:24:31,710
that no one has
thought of before.

574
00:24:31,710 --> 00:24:34,770
But the other thing that I
was interested to learn when

575
00:24:34,770 --> 00:24:37,020
I started working in
this space is actually

576
00:24:37,020 --> 00:24:39,240
there's a lot of drugs
that have progressed

577
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:42,570
in various clinical trials, and
they fail because there's just

578
00:24:42,570 --> 00:24:43,350
a mixed response.

579
00:24:43,350 --> 00:24:45,850
For some patients they work
really well, for other patients,

580
00:24:45,850 --> 00:24:47,110
they don't work at all.

581
00:24:47,110 --> 00:24:49,950
And we don't have the right
way of targeting the right drug

582
00:24:49,950 --> 00:24:51,280
to the right patient.

583
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:55,120
So for both of these, the
thing you would want is,

584
00:24:55,120 --> 00:24:57,030
again, a world model
that can help you

585
00:24:57,030 --> 00:24:58,780
simulate the answer
to the question.

586
00:24:58,780 --> 00:25:01,660
Given what you know about the
world of this patient's biology,

587
00:25:01,660 --> 00:25:05,580
if I take this action, does
the tumor go away or not?

588
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:09,900
So here's the oversimplified
schematic again of, you want

589
00:25:09,900 --> 00:25:12,600
something that tells you,
given a patient's data,

590
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:14,558
you want to be able to
simulate kind of rapidly

591
00:25:14,558 --> 00:25:16,142
a bunch of different
treatment options

592
00:25:16,142 --> 00:25:18,280
and get a reasonable
prediction of whether they'll

593
00:25:18,280 --> 00:25:21,250
be effective or ineffective.

594
00:25:21,250 --> 00:25:23,770
So our approach to
this is, first, we

595
00:25:23,770 --> 00:25:26,900
need to deploy our sensors
and collect a bunch of data.

596
00:25:26,900 --> 00:25:31,670
This is a video of our lab up in
South San Francisco in action.

597
00:25:31,670 --> 00:25:34,720
So what we do is we
get human lung tumor

598
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:38,450
specimens and a few other
tumors from a few other organs.

599
00:25:38,450 --> 00:25:41,380
These numbers are also a little
bit outdated at this point,

600
00:25:41,380 --> 00:25:43,880
but we source our tissue blocks.

601
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:45,830
We have a lab that
processes them.

602
00:25:45,830 --> 00:25:49,090
We punch out these
cores from the tissue

603
00:25:49,090 --> 00:25:51,147
that are about 1
millimeter in diameter.

604
00:25:51,147 --> 00:25:53,230
So they're very tiny but
I'll show you in a second

605
00:25:53,230 --> 00:25:55,990
that there's an unthinkable
amount of data packaged

606
00:25:55,990 --> 00:25:58,990
in that small 1 millimeter core.

607
00:25:58,990 --> 00:26:01,510
And then we will
basically put those

608
00:26:01,510 --> 00:26:04,408
through four different
data processing pipelines

609
00:26:04,408 --> 00:26:05,700
that I'll show you in a second.

610
00:26:05,700 --> 00:26:08,800

611
00:26:08,800 --> 00:26:12,340
One of those pipelines,
which is a fairly common one

612
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:16,450
to run on tissue samples, is
we collect these H&E images.

613
00:26:16,450 --> 00:26:18,540
H&E has the benefit
of being relatively

614
00:26:18,540 --> 00:26:21,130
cheap and easy to acquire,
and a lot of people have it.

615
00:26:21,130 --> 00:26:23,700
So most hospitals
and research centers

616
00:26:23,700 --> 00:26:27,060
are sitting on a ton of H&E.
There's a lot of public H&E.

617
00:26:27,060 --> 00:26:30,070
And if you want to do machine
learning on biological data,

618
00:26:30,070 --> 00:26:32,135
it basically looks
already like images,

619
00:26:32,135 --> 00:26:33,760
so you kind of know
what to do with it.

620
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:37,088
It's like a three channel RGB
image that you can work with.

621
00:26:37,088 --> 00:26:38,880
Technically, it's like
a two channel thing,

622
00:26:38,880 --> 00:26:40,330
because there's two
elements of the stain,

623
00:26:40,330 --> 00:26:42,220
but you can deconvolve
them if you want.

624
00:26:42,220 --> 00:26:45,030
It's not super relevant
to the rest of this talk.

625
00:26:45,030 --> 00:26:48,300
The other thing that we do
is, instead of just looking

626
00:26:48,300 --> 00:26:51,180
at the gross morphology, which
is what you mostly get out

627
00:26:51,180 --> 00:26:56,010
of this H&E stain, is we
have a 16-plex protein

628
00:26:56,010 --> 00:26:57,730
immunofluorescence panel.

629
00:26:57,730 --> 00:27:01,090
So that means that we're
able to detect proteins,

630
00:27:01,090 --> 00:27:03,570
16 different kinds of
proteins, by designing

631
00:27:03,570 --> 00:27:05,560
fluorescent antibodies
that bind to them.

632
00:27:05,560 --> 00:27:09,400
And we can see the
composition of this tissue.

633
00:27:09,400 --> 00:27:11,910
So in this pseudocolor
map what I'm showing

634
00:27:11,910 --> 00:27:15,890
is green, our T-cells, which
are part of your immune system.

635
00:27:15,890 --> 00:27:18,010
Blue is B-cells,
which is another part

636
00:27:18,010 --> 00:27:19,250
of your immune system.

637
00:27:19,250 --> 00:27:21,590
And then the red blob in
the middle is a tumor.

638
00:27:21,590 --> 00:27:24,910
So we're able to go ask what's
actually in this sample.

639
00:27:24,910 --> 00:27:28,690
This is kind of an RGB image
but with 16 channels instead

640
00:27:28,690 --> 00:27:30,700
of three channels.

641
00:27:30,700 --> 00:27:32,530
The other thing to
call out here is

642
00:27:32,530 --> 00:27:35,210
you'll notice that the
white box has moved with us.

643
00:27:35,210 --> 00:27:37,070
So these are spatially
aligned samples.

644
00:27:37,070 --> 00:27:40,023
And if you remember what I was
saying about RGB and depth,

645
00:27:40,023 --> 00:27:41,690
you might already be
thinking, oh, well,

646
00:27:41,690 --> 00:27:43,210
you could just
staple these together

647
00:27:43,210 --> 00:27:47,530
in the channel dimension and do
multimodal learning that way.

648
00:27:47,530 --> 00:27:51,010
One of the cooler kinds of
data that we have is spatial

649
00:27:51,010 --> 00:27:51,980
transcriptomics.

650
00:27:51,980 --> 00:27:54,710
So on, again, these
exact same samples,

651
00:27:54,710 --> 00:27:57,280
you'll see the white box
is moving with us again.

652
00:27:57,280 --> 00:28:00,890
We are able to detect
in 1,000-plex panel.

653
00:28:00,890 --> 00:28:03,520
So we're looking for 1,000
different genes worth of RNA

654
00:28:03,520 --> 00:28:04,490
transcripts.

655
00:28:04,490 --> 00:28:06,740
And those transcripts
are spatially localized.

656
00:28:06,740 --> 00:28:09,610
So we don't have to boil
down the sample into soup

657
00:28:09,610 --> 00:28:12,650
before feeding it in and asking
which RNA is in the sample.

658
00:28:12,650 --> 00:28:15,460
We get an XY co-ordinate and--

659
00:28:15,460 --> 00:28:18,460
well, it's really like
an XY by gene tuple

660
00:28:18,460 --> 00:28:23,210
that tells us this gene has
RNA expressed at this location.

661
00:28:23,210 --> 00:28:26,620
And you'll get anywhere
between a few thousand

662
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:28,420
to a few million
such transcripts

663
00:28:28,420 --> 00:28:31,000
in just 1 millimeter
of data, which

664
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:34,540
I'll try to show in a second if
it doesn't crash my computer.

665
00:28:34,540 --> 00:28:36,245
This data is very rich.

666
00:28:36,245 --> 00:28:37,370
It's also very complicated.

667
00:28:37,370 --> 00:28:38,450
It's also very expensive.

668
00:28:38,450 --> 00:28:41,900
So collecting this
data is the hardest

669
00:28:41,900 --> 00:28:44,510
and it's the thing that
the fewest people have.

670
00:28:44,510 --> 00:28:46,940
In fact, on one of
the previous slides,

671
00:28:46,940 --> 00:28:50,500
I had mentioned that we at
Noetik estimate that we have

672
00:28:50,500 --> 00:28:54,280
well over 1% and probably
over 2% of the world's spatial

673
00:28:54,280 --> 00:28:56,450
transcriptomics data
using this platform,

674
00:28:56,450 --> 00:28:58,960
which is called CosMc's.

675
00:28:58,960 --> 00:29:01,520
And the last thing we do
is genetic sequencing.

676
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:03,800
We do whole exome sequencing
of the patient's data

677
00:29:03,800 --> 00:29:05,980
so we know if they have
genetic mutations that

678
00:29:05,980 --> 00:29:07,897
could be relevant to
understanding their tumor

679
00:29:07,897 --> 00:29:09,070
biology.

680
00:29:09,070 --> 00:29:11,870
So this is the data
that lives out there.

681
00:29:11,870 --> 00:29:14,777
And the question is, how are
we going to learn from it?

682
00:29:14,777 --> 00:29:17,110
Before we do that, I just
want to talk a little bit more

683
00:29:17,110 --> 00:29:18,820
about the spatial
transcriptomics data,

684
00:29:18,820 --> 00:29:21,160
because it's so cool.

685
00:29:21,160 --> 00:29:25,300
So these are what the raw
transcripts look like in 3D.

686
00:29:25,300 --> 00:29:28,520
This is-- we do actually
detect them in X, Y and Z,

687
00:29:28,520 --> 00:29:31,670
but the Z-plane is 4
micrometers, 4 microns.

688
00:29:31,670 --> 00:29:33,140
So it's very, very thin.

689
00:29:33,140 --> 00:29:34,700
It's effectively just XY.

690
00:29:34,700 --> 00:29:38,380
And the colors here
are different genes.

691
00:29:38,380 --> 00:29:41,560
I'm going to try to run a live
demo of just what this raw data

692
00:29:41,560 --> 00:29:45,550
looks like, because it's a lot
of fun to play around with,

693
00:29:45,550 --> 00:29:48,190
but there's an
overwhelming amount

694
00:29:48,190 --> 00:29:52,180
of data jammed into this 1
millimeter diameter chunk

695
00:29:52,180 --> 00:29:52,695
of tissue.

696
00:29:52,695 --> 00:30:13,060

697
00:30:13,060 --> 00:30:15,260
I promise this computer
is not underpowered.

698
00:30:15,260 --> 00:30:17,270
It's just loading
11 million points.

699
00:30:17,270 --> 00:30:20,380
So this is what it looks
like at the micro level.

700
00:30:20,380 --> 00:30:22,780
You have this
incredibly rich tissue,

701
00:30:22,780 --> 00:30:27,010
and we are actually
showing here one

702
00:30:27,010 --> 00:30:29,570
of the first whole transcriptome
runs we've been able to do.

703
00:30:29,570 --> 00:30:31,670
So instead of detecting
1,000 gene panel,

704
00:30:31,670 --> 00:30:34,940
we're able to try to
detect over 18,000 genes.

705
00:30:34,940 --> 00:30:38,290
And we ran out of colors
after the first 20

706
00:30:38,290 --> 00:30:40,510
or So but you'll have
to take my word for it.

707
00:30:40,510 --> 00:30:42,230
So what the colors denote here?

708
00:30:42,230 --> 00:30:44,500
The colors are different genes.

709
00:30:44,500 --> 00:30:47,320
Yeah.

710
00:30:47,320 --> 00:30:48,050
OK.

711
00:30:48,050 --> 00:30:50,560
I will spare my
computer from trying

712
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:52,390
to render that for too long.

713
00:30:52,390 --> 00:30:55,820
Here's another example
from our 1,000-plex panel,

714
00:30:55,820 --> 00:30:58,560
just to give you another sense
of the kind of data that we work

715
00:30:58,560 --> 00:30:59,060
with.

716
00:30:59,060 --> 00:31:03,410
So at the local scale, now we're
talking about tons of microns.

717
00:31:03,410 --> 00:31:05,830
You have these highly
localized reads

718
00:31:05,830 --> 00:31:09,560
of what RNA is being
transcribed where in the cell.

719
00:31:09,560 --> 00:31:11,090
And I'll try to
render the labels,

720
00:31:11,090 --> 00:31:14,370
but again, the plotting software
struggles a bit with it.

721
00:31:14,370 --> 00:31:17,170

722
00:31:17,170 --> 00:31:18,533
OK, that's kind of an aside.

723
00:31:18,533 --> 00:31:20,950
I just think those data are
so cool that I like showing it

724
00:31:20,950 --> 00:31:22,690
whenever I have the chance.

725
00:31:22,690 --> 00:31:24,130
OK, so let me talk
about how we're

726
00:31:24,130 --> 00:31:27,250
trying to model this data
and the kinds of world models

727
00:31:27,250 --> 00:31:28,820
that we're trying to construct.

728
00:31:28,820 --> 00:31:31,940
So we have thousands,
at this point,

729
00:31:31,940 --> 00:31:35,690
over 10,000 cores from
different patients.

730
00:31:35,690 --> 00:31:38,110
Each of those cores is one of
those 1 millimeter diameter

731
00:31:38,110 --> 00:31:39,110
circles.

732
00:31:39,110 --> 00:31:41,450
In each core there will
be thousands of cells,

733
00:31:41,450 --> 00:31:43,390
and we can assign these
transcripts to cells

734
00:31:43,390 --> 00:31:46,490
with a segmentation algorithm
to say, in a given cell,

735
00:31:46,490 --> 00:31:50,980
you can count up how often do
you get a read of this gene CD3E

736
00:31:50,980 --> 00:31:52,730
within the borders of a cell.

737
00:31:52,730 --> 00:31:55,610
And So the data you get
is effectively tabular.

738
00:31:55,610 --> 00:31:56,960
It's also very, very sparse.

739
00:31:56,960 --> 00:32:00,160
Most cells don't
express most genes.

740
00:32:00,160 --> 00:32:03,340
We're going to try to model
this so that we can simulate

741
00:32:03,340 --> 00:32:05,620
things and ask,
well, does this tilt

742
00:32:05,620 --> 00:32:08,410
the tumor immune
microenvironment in a more

743
00:32:08,410 --> 00:32:10,990
or less favorable direction?

744
00:32:10,990 --> 00:32:14,470
Our general approach is to
use masked autoencoders.

745
00:32:14,470 --> 00:32:17,060
This ends up being a
transformer backbone.

746
00:32:17,060 --> 00:32:19,210
The thing worth calling
out is that it's not

747
00:32:19,210 --> 00:32:21,950
autoregressive
transformer processing,

748
00:32:21,950 --> 00:32:25,930
so there's no real sequence
to the genes to care about.

749
00:32:25,930 --> 00:32:27,800
It's more like masked
language modeling,

750
00:32:27,800 --> 00:32:29,960
where what we'll do
is take the input,

751
00:32:29,960 --> 00:32:32,060
we'll mask out
some of the tokens,

752
00:32:32,060 --> 00:32:33,763
and we predict the
tokens we mask out.

753
00:32:33,763 --> 00:32:35,680
I'll show on the next
slide what that actually

754
00:32:35,680 --> 00:32:37,690
looks like operationally.

755
00:32:37,690 --> 00:32:40,900
So if this is your input,
imagine this has 1,000 rows,

756
00:32:40,900 --> 00:32:43,180
soon 18,000 rows.

757
00:32:43,180 --> 00:32:44,680
You have every gene
and how often it

758
00:32:44,680 --> 00:32:46,400
was detected in a single cell.

759
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:48,730
So the input is one cell here.

760
00:32:48,730 --> 00:32:51,790
We have a tokenization
process that

761
00:32:51,790 --> 00:32:56,590
assigns a token to each
combination of gene identity

762
00:32:56,590 --> 00:32:58,490
and the expression level.

763
00:32:58,490 --> 00:33:01,340
So you're encoding both how
much of that thing you have,

764
00:33:01,340 --> 00:33:04,060
and also what the
identity of it is.

765
00:33:04,060 --> 00:33:07,100
And then we do partial masking.

766
00:33:07,100 --> 00:33:10,240
In our work, we tend to do
pretty aggressive masking.

767
00:33:10,240 --> 00:33:11,990
So the number you
should have in your head

768
00:33:11,990 --> 00:33:14,630
is like north of
90% of the tokens

769
00:33:14,630 --> 00:33:18,680
are removed and replaced
with a learned mask token.

770
00:33:18,680 --> 00:33:22,500
So almost everything is
removed from the sample

771
00:33:22,500 --> 00:33:26,400
and the job of the model is to
predict the things we removed.

772
00:33:26,400 --> 00:33:28,770
This is intentionally
a very hard task.

773
00:33:28,770 --> 00:33:31,730
We want it to be basically
as difficult as possible

774
00:33:31,730 --> 00:33:33,510
while still learning
the biology,

775
00:33:33,510 --> 00:33:37,160
because if you are able
to do this, what it means

776
00:33:37,160 --> 00:33:40,010
is that you're able to use a
very small amount of context

777
00:33:40,010 --> 00:33:43,950
to infer, OK, well, I
see I have a lot of CD3.

778
00:33:43,950 --> 00:33:46,460
I don't get to see
how much CD8 I have

779
00:33:46,460 --> 00:33:48,990
because that got masked
out in the masking process.

780
00:33:48,990 --> 00:33:51,710
So I have to GUESS based
on my knowledge of biology

781
00:33:51,710 --> 00:33:54,350
from learning from this
data set, is it likely

782
00:33:54,350 --> 00:33:58,580
that I express CD8, which would
make me a cytotoxic or a killer

783
00:33:58,580 --> 00:34:01,460
T-cell if I express CD3,
which is kind of a more

784
00:34:01,460 --> 00:34:03,770
generic marker for T-cells
that may or may not

785
00:34:03,770 --> 00:34:04,820
be killer T-cells.

786
00:34:04,820 --> 00:34:08,300

787
00:34:08,300 --> 00:34:11,389
At inference time, what
you can do with this model

788
00:34:11,389 --> 00:34:14,179
is provide a couple
of things that are not

789
00:34:14,179 --> 00:34:17,070
masked, like two tokens
worth of information,

790
00:34:17,070 --> 00:34:19,760
and then staple on a
bunch of mask tokens,

791
00:34:19,760 --> 00:34:22,440
the other 998 genes
in this panel.

792
00:34:22,440 --> 00:34:25,530
And then you can predict
everything that's masked out.

793
00:34:25,530 --> 00:34:27,320
So you can say,
given that you're

794
00:34:27,320 --> 00:34:29,420
conditioned on
knowing you are T-cell

795
00:34:29,420 --> 00:34:32,190
and you're not a tumor, what
else do you think is happening?

796
00:34:32,190 --> 00:34:35,790
And if you've learned
from this huge data set,

797
00:34:35,790 --> 00:34:38,478
you might think maybe the best
you can do is say, well, OK,

798
00:34:38,478 --> 00:34:40,020
what is the average
T-cell look like?

799
00:34:40,020 --> 00:34:43,639
Because all I've told you is
you have this T-cell marker CD3,

800
00:34:43,639 --> 00:34:45,409
and you're definitely
not a tumor cell,

801
00:34:45,409 --> 00:34:48,440
because you're expressing
none of this keratin marker.

802
00:34:48,440 --> 00:34:50,357
I don't want to say that
this model is boring.

803
00:34:50,357 --> 00:34:52,607
There's a lot of interesting
stuff you can do with it,

804
00:34:52,607 --> 00:34:54,270
but it's distinctly
not multimodal

805
00:34:54,270 --> 00:34:55,710
and I promised
you multimodality.

806
00:34:55,710 --> 00:34:58,880
So I'm going to keep going
with this background in mind

807
00:34:58,880 --> 00:35:01,790
and tell you about one
model that we have gotten

808
00:35:01,790 --> 00:35:05,210
a lot of leverage out
of, which is to say,

809
00:35:05,210 --> 00:35:07,873
this task is very hard and
there's a lot of ambiguity.

810
00:35:07,873 --> 00:35:09,290
And if you remember,
I was talking

811
00:35:09,290 --> 00:35:11,420
about multimodality
as a way to resolve

812
00:35:11,420 --> 00:35:13,230
ambiguity in predictions.

813
00:35:13,230 --> 00:35:16,040
So one thing that
we do is we will

814
00:35:16,040 --> 00:35:17,660
take the local
spatial neighborhood

815
00:35:17,660 --> 00:35:20,580
of a cell, the one we're trying
to make predictions about.

816
00:35:20,580 --> 00:35:23,070
We'll ask, who are your
eight nearest neighbors?

817
00:35:23,070 --> 00:35:25,100
And we will force
the information

818
00:35:25,100 --> 00:35:27,860
about what those eight nearest
neighbors are expressing

819
00:35:27,860 --> 00:35:29,480
through a bottleneck.

820
00:35:29,480 --> 00:35:32,250
That bottleneck is just
another transformer,

821
00:35:32,250 --> 00:35:35,420
but it is required to
boil down basically

822
00:35:35,420 --> 00:35:37,940
all of that rich spatial
context about what's

823
00:35:37,940 --> 00:35:41,130
happening in the local
environment down into one token.

824
00:35:41,130 --> 00:35:44,400
And then we use this
adaptive LayerNorm approach,

825
00:35:44,400 --> 00:35:47,100
or we've tried the
append tokens thing.

826
00:35:47,100 --> 00:35:48,570
We've tried cross-attention.

827
00:35:48,570 --> 00:35:49,800
They all seem to work.

828
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:52,010
Some are just more
efficient than others.

829
00:35:52,010 --> 00:35:55,310
And that feeds in as an
additional input to what

830
00:35:55,310 --> 00:35:56,970
we refer to as the backbone.

831
00:35:56,970 --> 00:36:00,770
So now, if you have a
prompt in your training

832
00:36:00,770 --> 00:36:03,260
that's ambiguous, you
have a little bit of help,

833
00:36:03,260 --> 00:36:05,780
because you might
say, well, I don't

834
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:08,990
know if I'm a killer T-cell or
not, but seven of my neighbors

835
00:36:08,990 --> 00:36:09,960
are killer T-cells.

836
00:36:09,960 --> 00:36:12,410
Maybe I'm also a killer
T-cell, and that should help

837
00:36:12,410 --> 00:36:13,710
you do a little bit better.

838
00:36:13,710 --> 00:36:16,220
And the thing that I should
have added here and forgot

839
00:36:16,220 --> 00:36:18,860
is showing that if you're
just monitoring the training

840
00:36:18,860 --> 00:36:20,780
loss of the backbone,
it really likes

841
00:36:20,780 --> 00:36:22,050
having the spatial context.

842
00:36:22,050 --> 00:36:24,565
It helps quite a lot in
lowering that loss curve.

843
00:36:24,565 --> 00:36:28,400

844
00:36:28,400 --> 00:36:30,380
Once you have a
model like that, you

845
00:36:30,380 --> 00:36:33,270
can do very large scale
inference with it.

846
00:36:33,270 --> 00:36:37,920
So what we will do is take
a real patient sample.

847
00:36:37,920 --> 00:36:40,320
We'll pick a location
in that sample,

848
00:36:40,320 --> 00:36:42,240
look up the eight
nearest neighbors,

849
00:36:42,240 --> 00:36:45,320
and then we will ask the
model to do inference

850
00:36:45,320 --> 00:36:47,130
with a specified prompt.

851
00:36:47,130 --> 00:36:50,160
In this case, I'm telling
it, you are a killer T-cell.

852
00:36:50,160 --> 00:36:53,270
You express these markers
for regular T-cells

853
00:36:53,270 --> 00:36:55,970
and for cytotoxicity,
and I'm asking

854
00:36:55,970 --> 00:36:59,730
it to predict the other
990 plus genes conditioned

855
00:36:59,730 --> 00:37:01,260
on the spatial context.

856
00:37:01,260 --> 00:37:04,890
And on the right, I'm going to
slowly build up at each position

857
00:37:04,890 --> 00:37:07,140
here what the model
thinks is happening

858
00:37:07,140 --> 00:37:10,350
for one such gene, IL7R.

859
00:37:10,350 --> 00:37:12,480
And you will already
get a sense once this

860
00:37:12,480 --> 00:37:17,220
starts to speed up, of how many
simulations we're doing here.

861
00:37:17,220 --> 00:37:19,710
As of last week,
I think we had run

862
00:37:19,710 --> 00:37:22,260
on the order of 6
billion virtual cell

863
00:37:22,260 --> 00:37:23,500
simulations like this.

864
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:29,670
So we are able to ask basically
arbitrary queries about what

865
00:37:29,670 --> 00:37:32,800
is happening in patient tissue
by saying, hypothetically,

866
00:37:32,800 --> 00:37:36,130
if this cell type were in this
location in the patient tissue,

867
00:37:36,130 --> 00:37:38,080
what do I think it's doing?

868
00:37:38,080 --> 00:37:39,893
And to close the
loop in a second,

869
00:37:39,893 --> 00:37:41,310
I'll tell you about
how you can do

870
00:37:41,310 --> 00:37:43,860
counterfactual simulations
that will help you

871
00:37:43,860 --> 00:37:45,850
think about potential drugs.

872
00:37:45,850 --> 00:37:49,110
But for now, just
appreciating that once

873
00:37:49,110 --> 00:37:51,610
you have this additional
input stream to the model,

874
00:37:51,610 --> 00:37:53,460
you can pull out
a lot of richness

875
00:37:53,460 --> 00:37:56,570
about what it's learned about
how spatial context affects

876
00:37:56,570 --> 00:37:58,735
what is happening at
the single cell level.

877
00:37:58,735 --> 00:38:01,760

878
00:38:01,760 --> 00:38:04,460
We built a little
web UI that lets

879
00:38:04,460 --> 00:38:06,240
you explore some of this data.

880
00:38:06,240 --> 00:38:08,600
I won't spend too
long in it, but it

881
00:38:08,600 --> 00:38:12,360
lets you pick a cell type, fly
around a real patient sample,

882
00:38:12,360 --> 00:38:15,650
see what the cell identities
of those nearest neighbors

883
00:38:15,650 --> 00:38:19,910
is, and then see how the model's
prediction of different genes

884
00:38:19,910 --> 00:38:22,830
changes as you move
around the space.

885
00:38:22,830 --> 00:38:26,820
So I could spend all day kind
of mucking around in this,

886
00:38:26,820 --> 00:38:27,958
but it's public.

887
00:38:27,958 --> 00:38:29,000
Feel free to poke around.

888
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:31,167
Let us know if you find
anything interesting that we

889
00:38:31,167 --> 00:38:34,130
should turn into a drug.

890
00:38:34,130 --> 00:38:35,270
Celleporter?

891
00:38:35,270 --> 00:38:38,180
Yeah, the link is
celleporter.ai,

892
00:38:38,180 --> 00:38:42,320
as we think of it as teleporting
cells around in patient data.

893
00:38:42,320 --> 00:38:43,380
May I ask a question?

894
00:38:43,380 --> 00:38:43,880
Sure.

895
00:38:43,880 --> 00:38:47,150
So masking seems like a
really interesting approach

896
00:38:47,150 --> 00:38:48,650
and strategy to apply.

897
00:38:48,650 --> 00:38:53,280
Is that to narrow it down to
that one particular pathway?

898
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:57,330
Is [INAUDIBLE] 90% masking
so you can actually

899
00:38:57,330 --> 00:38:58,720
trace every single one?

900
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:00,930
Is that the core
function of masking?

901
00:39:00,930 --> 00:39:02,890
I think it buys us two things.

902
00:39:02,890 --> 00:39:04,780
The first is that
at training time,

903
00:39:04,780 --> 00:39:06,828
it makes the task
very, very hard so

904
00:39:06,828 --> 00:39:08,370
that the only way
you could answer it

905
00:39:08,370 --> 00:39:11,320
is by learning about
fundamental patient biology.

906
00:39:11,320 --> 00:39:13,930
If you provided-- go
to the other extreme,

907
00:39:13,930 --> 00:39:15,550
you provide 999 genes.

908
00:39:15,550 --> 00:39:16,870
You only need to predict one.

909
00:39:16,870 --> 00:39:18,730
You will probably
identify that, oh,

910
00:39:18,730 --> 00:39:21,000
I can usually predict this
gene by the combination

911
00:39:21,000 --> 00:39:22,150
of these other two.

912
00:39:22,150 --> 00:39:23,610
The other thing
that it helps us do

913
00:39:23,610 --> 00:39:26,490
is, I'll show this in
a second, but if you

914
00:39:26,490 --> 00:39:30,100
train with a very high masking
ratio, at inference time,

915
00:39:30,100 --> 00:39:32,790
you can bump the
masking ratio to 100%

916
00:39:32,790 --> 00:39:34,228
and the model doesn't freak out.

917
00:39:34,228 --> 00:39:36,520
It's like, oh, yeah, I'm used
to seeing almost nothing.

918
00:39:36,520 --> 00:39:38,740
And then you can do
interesting things like, well,

919
00:39:38,740 --> 00:39:41,130
what if I give you no
information about that center

920
00:39:41,130 --> 00:39:44,880
cell and you only get to use
the local spatial context, which

921
00:39:44,880 --> 00:39:47,740
is a mode we actually
operate in quite often.

922
00:39:47,740 --> 00:39:48,240
Yeah.

923
00:39:48,240 --> 00:39:51,090

924
00:39:51,090 --> 00:39:53,610
OK, this is another
kind of thing

925
00:39:53,610 --> 00:39:55,900
we would do with
these virtual cells.

926
00:39:55,900 --> 00:40:01,590
So the thing that I
showed on this slide

927
00:40:01,590 --> 00:40:04,390
is the surrounding
context is real data.

928
00:40:04,390 --> 00:40:07,020
We go to a real location
and a real patient's tissue,

929
00:40:07,020 --> 00:40:09,910
look at 8 nearest cells,
feed that expression in.

930
00:40:09,910 --> 00:40:11,880
But the model doesn't
know what's real data

931
00:40:11,880 --> 00:40:14,850
and what isn't, so you can go
create a synthetic neighborhood

932
00:40:14,850 --> 00:40:17,860
and say, OK, I'm going to
start from the real data,

933
00:40:17,860 --> 00:40:19,800
and I'm going to simulate
the effect of a drug

934
00:40:19,800 --> 00:40:22,320
by knocking down a particular
target I'm interested in.

935
00:40:22,320 --> 00:40:23,940
And say the neighborhood
is basically

936
00:40:23,940 --> 00:40:27,940
the same, except that I've
removed a target of interest.

937
00:40:27,940 --> 00:40:31,090
I've done a synthetic knockout
in the surrounding context,

938
00:40:31,090 --> 00:40:34,000
and I want to know when
I do that knockout,

939
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:36,210
do I make this
hypothetical center

940
00:40:36,210 --> 00:40:39,400
T-cell more or less likely
to be in tumor killing mode?

941
00:40:39,400 --> 00:40:42,000
And that's what this
animation shows.

942
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:44,380
The gene identities
are redacted,

943
00:40:44,380 --> 00:40:48,180
but the y-axis is the
increase in the production

944
00:40:48,180 --> 00:40:51,270
or predicted production
of granzyme K, which

945
00:40:51,270 --> 00:40:56,770
is part of the T-cells cytotoxic
arsenal for attacking tumors.

946
00:40:56,770 --> 00:41:01,020
And we can do these simulations
for each of these 10,000 plus

947
00:41:01,020 --> 00:41:04,330
patient samples for each of
the 1,000 genes in the panel.

948
00:41:04,330 --> 00:41:06,760
And you start to see how,
with the right scale,

949
00:41:06,760 --> 00:41:10,050
you can very quickly search over
hypotheses for what's happening

950
00:41:10,050 --> 00:41:13,080
in the patient tissue.

951
00:41:13,080 --> 00:41:14,220
Yeah?

952
00:41:14,220 --> 00:41:20,430
How will this [INAUDIBLE] be
updated for 20,000 or 7000--

953
00:41:20,430 --> 00:41:21,210
Yeah.

954
00:41:21,210 --> 00:41:34,560
So maybe a year
or so [INAUDIBLE]

955
00:41:34,560 --> 00:41:35,280
Yeah.

956
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:35,890
Good question.

957
00:41:35,890 --> 00:41:38,940
So the question is 8,000
patients worth of data enough

958
00:41:38,940 --> 00:41:40,420
or do you need more?

959
00:41:40,420 --> 00:41:42,760
[INAUDIBLE]

960
00:41:42,760 --> 00:41:43,260
Yeah.

961
00:41:43,260 --> 00:41:44,843
Could you augment
with synthetic data?

962
00:41:44,843 --> 00:41:48,690
I think the answer is maybe.

963
00:41:48,690 --> 00:41:51,880
I would say if you are staying
within a single indication,

964
00:41:51,880 --> 00:41:54,940
like we often think about
non-small cell lung cancer,

965
00:41:54,940 --> 00:41:57,660
it's possible 8,000 patients
covers the diversity

966
00:41:57,660 --> 00:42:00,720
of different patient biology or
different modes that the tumor

967
00:42:00,720 --> 00:42:03,558
might be using to escape
the immune system.

968
00:42:03,558 --> 00:42:05,850
I think there are things you
could learn from expanding

969
00:42:05,850 --> 00:42:07,800
to more indications,
different cancer

970
00:42:07,800 --> 00:42:11,105
types and different organs
that could be generalizable,

971
00:42:11,105 --> 00:42:13,510
or even back to non-small
cell lung cancer.

972
00:42:13,510 --> 00:42:15,630
And So we are actively
collecting right now

973
00:42:15,630 --> 00:42:18,090
more data and more
indications and more organs

974
00:42:18,090 --> 00:42:21,600
to make sure we're not ignoring
a pocket of how the tumors might

975
00:42:21,600 --> 00:42:23,940
be learning to evade
the immune system.

976
00:42:23,940 --> 00:42:28,740
Can we generate theoretically
so that everything

977
00:42:28,740 --> 00:42:34,830
would be able to assume
[INAUDIBLE] is the case for all

978
00:42:34,830 --> 00:42:39,370
of us to have a sense
of [INAUDIBLE] scalable

979
00:42:39,370 --> 00:42:41,940
[INAUDIBLE]?

980
00:42:41,940 --> 00:42:43,530
Yeah.

981
00:42:43,530 --> 00:42:47,280
I think there probably are ways
to generate synthetic data.

982
00:42:47,280 --> 00:42:50,950
I'm not sure learning from
it directly would help.

983
00:42:50,950 --> 00:42:53,560
If you're using a model that
has trained on real data,

984
00:42:53,560 --> 00:42:56,220
I'm not sure if it's going to
do better when it's generating

985
00:42:56,220 --> 00:42:58,380
its own synthetic
data, but there

986
00:42:58,380 --> 00:43:00,270
are ways you can use
your own knowledge

987
00:43:00,270 --> 00:43:02,333
about the biological
system to produce things

988
00:43:02,333 --> 00:43:03,750
like these synthetic
neighborhoods

989
00:43:03,750 --> 00:43:06,893
where you're saying, I'm going
to go make a targeted change

990
00:43:06,893 --> 00:43:09,060
and then run inference and
see what the model thinks

991
00:43:09,060 --> 00:43:11,820
will happen when I do a
very specific perturbation

992
00:43:11,820 --> 00:43:13,620
in the data.

993
00:43:13,620 --> 00:43:15,140
[INAUDIBLE]

994
00:43:15,140 --> 00:43:15,640
Thanks.

995
00:43:15,640 --> 00:43:16,140
OK.

996
00:43:16,140 --> 00:43:18,804

997
00:43:18,804 --> 00:43:21,240
The thing about this data
that I mentioned earlier

998
00:43:21,240 --> 00:43:25,050
is it's expensive, and it's
rare, and it's hard to collect.

999
00:43:25,050 --> 00:43:28,423
I also mentioned that this
H&E data is image-based,

1000
00:43:28,423 --> 00:43:30,840
it's pretty cheap, it's fairly
ubiquitous, a lot of people

1001
00:43:30,840 --> 00:43:31,800
have it.

1002
00:43:31,800 --> 00:43:34,290
And one thing that we've
been doing increasingly

1003
00:43:34,290 --> 00:43:36,510
is seeing if we can
use our model, which

1004
00:43:36,510 --> 00:43:39,150
we call OCTO in part informally,
because it kind of looks

1005
00:43:39,150 --> 00:43:40,770
like an octopus
with a bunch of arms

1006
00:43:40,770 --> 00:43:45,330
that sample different
modalities to just translate

1007
00:43:45,330 --> 00:43:46,270
from one to the other.

1008
00:43:46,270 --> 00:43:49,320
So now, we're back to
multimodality as translation.

1009
00:43:49,320 --> 00:43:51,930
The way that you do
this is take this model

1010
00:43:51,930 --> 00:43:53,830
that we're now familiar with.

1011
00:43:53,830 --> 00:43:56,285
And now, this diagram
should look very familiar,

1012
00:43:56,285 --> 00:43:57,660
except instead of
gene expression

1013
00:43:57,660 --> 00:43:59,140
data from the
local neighborhood,

1014
00:43:59,140 --> 00:44:01,980
we're passing in
the surrounding H&E

1015
00:44:01,980 --> 00:44:04,950
morphology of what is
happening local to that cell

1016
00:44:04,950 --> 00:44:06,450
in the patient's tissue.

1017
00:44:06,450 --> 00:44:09,120
We can only do this, because the
samples are spatially aligned

1018
00:44:09,120 --> 00:44:10,690
as precisely as they are.

1019
00:44:10,690 --> 00:44:14,680
And now the model gets to use
this additional input as help.

1020
00:44:14,680 --> 00:44:17,165
It's saying, I'm facing
ambiguity, what can I do?

1021
00:44:17,165 --> 00:44:19,290
Oh, well, I can look at
the morphology of the cells

1022
00:44:19,290 --> 00:44:22,960
around me and try to understand
if that makes me, for example,

1023
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:24,780
a cytotoxic T-cell or not.

1024
00:44:24,780 --> 00:44:30,460
Once you've trained this model,
you can then go to full masking.

1025
00:44:30,460 --> 00:44:33,150
Just say, OK, I'm going to
effectively ablate the primary

1026
00:44:33,150 --> 00:44:36,640
input and just pass
in 1,000 mask tokens.

1027
00:44:36,640 --> 00:44:38,730
And now the only
thing the model can do

1028
00:44:38,730 --> 00:44:41,350
is use the local
spatial context to say,

1029
00:44:41,350 --> 00:44:43,050
well, I don't know
anything about what

1030
00:44:43,050 --> 00:44:44,140
the expression should be.

1031
00:44:44,140 --> 00:44:46,270
I have to use this
local context to try

1032
00:44:46,270 --> 00:44:48,340
to make a prediction
of what the gene

1033
00:44:48,340 --> 00:44:50,290
expression of a
hypothetical cell

1034
00:44:50,290 --> 00:44:52,730
at the center of that
spatial window is doing.

1035
00:44:52,730 --> 00:44:56,950
And because we've trained it
at a very high masking ratio,

1036
00:44:56,950 --> 00:44:58,940
the model is not totally
clueless about this.

1037
00:44:58,940 --> 00:45:01,880
And we were curious if it
makes reasonable predictions.

1038
00:45:01,880 --> 00:45:03,580
So what we will do
at inference time

1039
00:45:03,580 --> 00:45:06,410
is take one of these
H&E images, which again,

1040
00:45:06,410 --> 00:45:08,800
are fairly easy to acquire.

1041
00:45:08,800 --> 00:45:11,620
Pick a spatial window
and run the model forward

1042
00:45:11,620 --> 00:45:13,240
in inference mode
and say, OK, I'm

1043
00:45:13,240 --> 00:45:14,800
not going to tell you
anything about the expression

1044
00:45:14,800 --> 00:45:16,910
of the cell in this case,
because I don't have it.

1045
00:45:16,910 --> 00:45:18,560
We don't know what's going on.

1046
00:45:18,560 --> 00:45:20,500
There could be someone
sent me this H&E image

1047
00:45:20,500 --> 00:45:23,500
and they never ran
spatial transcriptomics.

1048
00:45:23,500 --> 00:45:27,820
The model will make a prediction
about what the expression of all

1049
00:45:27,820 --> 00:45:31,030
1,000 genes in our panel is
at the center of that spatial

1050
00:45:31,030 --> 00:45:31,730
window.

1051
00:45:31,730 --> 00:45:34,580
And here, it's giving me
some value for a keratin 19,

1052
00:45:34,580 --> 00:45:35,923
which is a tumor marker.

1053
00:45:35,923 --> 00:45:38,090
And you can just run this
as many times as you want.

1054
00:45:38,090 --> 00:45:41,190
So in practice, we'll
run this 1,000 times.

1055
00:45:41,190 --> 00:45:41,690
Yeah.

1056
00:45:41,690 --> 00:45:43,750
One question online is,
are you only predicting

1057
00:45:43,750 --> 00:45:47,270
the transcriptomics of
non-cancerous cells?

1058
00:45:47,270 --> 00:45:47,770
No.

1059
00:45:47,770 --> 00:45:51,410
We are predicting the
transcriptomics for every cell,

1060
00:45:51,410 --> 00:45:54,860
including immune cells,
tumor cells, fibroblasts,

1061
00:45:54,860 --> 00:45:56,800
stromal cells,
whatever's in the data.

1062
00:45:56,800 --> 00:45:58,960
Yeah.

1063
00:45:58,960 --> 00:46:01,580
So if you run this process
a few thousand times,

1064
00:46:01,580 --> 00:46:04,330
you get basically a prediction
heat map of where is it likely

1065
00:46:04,330 --> 00:46:07,030
that there's a tumor, because
you're asking the model

1066
00:46:07,030 --> 00:46:10,090
to predict one of the 1,000
things it's predicting at each

1067
00:46:10,090 --> 00:46:12,200
of these locations
is a tumor marker.

1068
00:46:12,200 --> 00:46:14,180
So here's the heat map you get.

1069
00:46:14,180 --> 00:46:16,970
And then, this is
now going back.

1070
00:46:16,970 --> 00:46:19,060
And for this core
we actually do have

1071
00:46:19,060 --> 00:46:21,590
cell typing from
transcriptomics data,

1072
00:46:21,590 --> 00:46:23,750
and the red dots are where
there's actually tumor.

1073
00:46:23,750 --> 00:46:25,240
And you can see
that the model is

1074
00:46:25,240 --> 00:46:29,440
predicting where there's tumor
markers pretty effectively.

1075
00:46:29,440 --> 00:46:30,980
I won't pretend this is perfect.

1076
00:46:30,980 --> 00:46:33,650
There are definitely examples
where it doesn't do a great job.

1077
00:46:33,650 --> 00:46:37,220
So here's another sample
that has a ton of B-cells.

1078
00:46:37,220 --> 00:46:39,080
That's the blue in this diagram.

1079
00:46:39,080 --> 00:46:42,130
And it does-- it mostly
predicts that there

1080
00:46:42,130 --> 00:46:45,710
isn't a lot of tumor marker,
which is mostly accurate.

1081
00:46:45,710 --> 00:46:47,570
There isn't a lot of
tumor in this sample,

1082
00:46:47,570 --> 00:46:50,540
but it's still missing the
few areas where there is.

1083
00:46:50,540 --> 00:46:53,060
But for other markers, even
within the same sample,

1084
00:46:53,060 --> 00:46:55,180
it's doing a very
good job of predicting

1085
00:46:55,180 --> 00:46:58,240
from just this
morphology image what

1086
00:46:58,240 --> 00:47:00,590
the expression of
various genes is.

1087
00:47:00,590 --> 00:47:05,620
And yeah, imagine 997 more
columns of this diagram and then

1088
00:47:05,620 --> 00:47:08,030
10,000 more slides
with other samples.

1089
00:47:08,030 --> 00:47:11,140
And that's how we're evaluating
the ability of this model

1090
00:47:11,140 --> 00:47:13,840
to do that translation
from the images

1091
00:47:13,840 --> 00:47:17,140
to the rich spatial
transcriptomics.

1092
00:47:17,140 --> 00:47:18,970
So essentially this
is what we call

1093
00:47:18,970 --> 00:47:23,570
the masking where the
[INAUDIBLE] images are cheap.

1094
00:47:23,570 --> 00:47:28,210
And So you train it on the
other set of images or picture,

1095
00:47:28,210 --> 00:47:30,730
and then you can generalize
on it [INAUDIBLE].

1096
00:47:30,730 --> 00:47:31,280
Right.

1097
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:33,530
We're leveraging the fact
that we have the paired data

1098
00:47:33,530 --> 00:47:37,010
in-house, so we can teach the
model to do that translation.

1099
00:47:37,010 --> 00:47:39,610
And then, at inference
time, the model

1100
00:47:39,610 --> 00:47:41,360
is happy if you
mask everything out.

1101
00:47:41,360 --> 00:47:42,100
It'll just use

1102
00:47:42,100 --> 00:47:44,080
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

1103
00:47:44,080 --> 00:47:47,530
--your synthetic data, because
now you can see the other one

1104
00:47:47,530 --> 00:47:50,300
is readily available
to you concerning that.

1105
00:47:50,300 --> 00:47:52,970
And then you can have
better prediction.

1106
00:47:52,970 --> 00:47:54,337
Yeah, totally.

1107
00:47:54,337 --> 00:47:56,920
So the thing I'm going to show
on the next slide is actually--

1108
00:47:56,920 --> 00:48:00,370
I've been playing with ideas
for what would an interface look

1109
00:48:00,370 --> 00:48:03,340
like for, you have H&E data,
you don't have the ability

1110
00:48:03,340 --> 00:48:05,530
to generate spatial
transcriptomics.

1111
00:48:05,530 --> 00:48:09,220
You can imagine an interface
where someone uploads their own

1112
00:48:09,220 --> 00:48:13,360
H&E. You predict a full 1,000
dimensional panel of what

1113
00:48:13,360 --> 00:48:15,970
the expression is of a bunch
of different genes at every

1114
00:48:15,970 --> 00:48:17,350
location.

1115
00:48:17,350 --> 00:48:20,600
We know what different
gene programs look like,

1116
00:48:20,600 --> 00:48:22,810
and you can actually use
a large language model

1117
00:48:22,810 --> 00:48:27,410
to find for you common areas
where things are popping out

1118
00:48:27,410 --> 00:48:29,090
and descriptions of
what's happening.

1119
00:48:29,090 --> 00:48:31,400
So you get a fully
end-to-end automated.

1120
00:48:31,400 --> 00:48:34,810
You upload a sample, we predict
what the gene expression would

1121
00:48:34,810 --> 00:48:36,550
be in that sample,
and then you can

1122
00:48:36,550 --> 00:48:39,190
go explore the data,
talk with an LLM,

1123
00:48:39,190 --> 00:48:40,730
Explorer, whatever you want.

1124
00:48:40,730 --> 00:48:42,580
Ask questions about
it and eventually run

1125
00:48:42,580 --> 00:48:44,750
simulations in browser.

1126
00:48:44,750 --> 00:48:47,110
So cool.

1127
00:48:47,110 --> 00:48:47,660
OK.

1128
00:48:47,660 --> 00:48:50,110
So basically what
we have built here

1129
00:48:50,110 --> 00:48:52,390
is a system that
attempts to translate

1130
00:48:52,390 --> 00:48:55,540
relatively easy to acquire
data into rich patient

1131
00:48:55,540 --> 00:48:57,250
representations.

1132
00:48:57,250 --> 00:49:01,480
This is kind of a pretty PCA
projection of the first few

1133
00:49:01,480 --> 00:49:04,750
principal components of the
1,000 dimensional signal at each

1134
00:49:04,750 --> 00:49:06,010
location.

1135
00:49:06,010 --> 00:49:09,190
But what we will actually
do with that is then

1136
00:49:09,190 --> 00:49:11,890
each dot in the
diagram to the right

1137
00:49:11,890 --> 00:49:15,310
is one sample from one
patient, and we're embedding it

1138
00:49:15,310 --> 00:49:16,820
in a two dimensional space.

1139
00:49:16,820 --> 00:49:18,520
In this case, I think
we're using t-SNE,

1140
00:49:18,520 --> 00:49:23,090
and we're putting
patient samples together

1141
00:49:23,090 --> 00:49:25,790
if their predicted gene
expression profiles are similar.

1142
00:49:25,790 --> 00:49:29,480
And what I'm labeling by here is
actually the patient genetics.

1143
00:49:29,480 --> 00:49:32,650
So the model has no idea
about whether a patient has

1144
00:49:32,650 --> 00:49:37,580
a genetic mutation in
cancer driver mutations.

1145
00:49:37,580 --> 00:49:39,820
But it turns out that,
because the model has learned

1146
00:49:39,820 --> 00:49:42,940
patient biology, when you
cluster together predictions

1147
00:49:42,940 --> 00:49:44,560
from different
patients, the ones

1148
00:49:44,560 --> 00:49:46,240
that have common
genetic mutations

1149
00:49:46,240 --> 00:49:48,110
end up clustering in this space.

1150
00:49:48,110 --> 00:49:51,400
So you can recover
patient biology

1151
00:49:51,400 --> 00:49:54,680
starting, again, just
from this H&E image,

1152
00:49:54,680 --> 00:49:57,640
because we have trained this
model on a bunch of paired data

1153
00:49:57,640 --> 00:50:01,270
that has H&E and
spatial transcriptomics.

1154
00:50:01,270 --> 00:50:03,970
[INAUDIBLE] interesting
classification stuff.

1155
00:50:03,970 --> 00:50:06,670
Yeah, we spend a
lot of time asking

1156
00:50:06,670 --> 00:50:10,840
what the structure of this space
is telling us about the data.

1157
00:50:10,840 --> 00:50:14,140
I'm just going to give a quick
tour of another model that

1158
00:50:14,140 --> 00:50:16,720
will look familiar,
because it's using

1159
00:50:16,720 --> 00:50:18,760
similar masked autoencoding.

1160
00:50:18,760 --> 00:50:20,570
This is using that protein data.

1161
00:50:20,570 --> 00:50:23,810
So in this example, the model is
not predicting gene expression,

1162
00:50:23,810 --> 00:50:25,640
it's predicting protein images.

1163
00:50:25,640 --> 00:50:28,480
And we're feeding in the
other three modalities

1164
00:50:28,480 --> 00:50:30,230
as extra tokens.

1165
00:50:30,230 --> 00:50:32,900
So this is now not the adaptive
LayerNorm thing I mentioned.

1166
00:50:32,900 --> 00:50:36,440
It's just stapling on bonus
tokens into the token stream

1167
00:50:36,440 --> 00:50:39,560
and letting them interact with
the primary token stream, which

1168
00:50:39,560 --> 00:50:44,000
comes from these 16
channel protein images.

1169
00:50:44,000 --> 00:50:47,655
So H&E is the data that
you are able to create?

1170
00:50:47,655 --> 00:50:48,155
We--

1171
00:50:48,155 --> 00:50:48,500
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

1172
00:50:48,500 --> 00:50:49,700
--also collect [INAUDIBLE].

1173
00:50:49,700 --> 00:50:51,350
We collect all of this.

1174
00:50:51,350 --> 00:50:52,040
All of it, OK.

1175
00:50:52,040 --> 00:50:53,420
Yeah.

1176
00:50:53,420 --> 00:50:57,180
So into this model, if I'm
creating a new data set,

1177
00:50:57,180 --> 00:51:00,950
given that earlier process,
can that become another input?

1178
00:51:00,950 --> 00:51:02,460
Yeah.

1179
00:51:02,460 --> 00:51:07,457
So you could only collect
H&E impute or predict

1180
00:51:07,457 --> 00:51:10,040
what the spatial transcriptomics
would have looked like if you

1181
00:51:10,040 --> 00:51:12,270
collected it, feed that in.

1182
00:51:12,270 --> 00:51:14,545
The question is, if that
helps you learn anything,

1183
00:51:14,545 --> 00:51:16,170
and I don't have an
answer to that yet.

1184
00:51:16,170 --> 00:51:19,910
We are exploring it but
it's an interesting idea.

1185
00:51:19,910 --> 00:51:20,520
OK.

1186
00:51:20,520 --> 00:51:24,450
So what we do in this space
is ask questions like, well,

1187
00:51:24,450 --> 00:51:26,930
how do we want to simulate
the effect of a drug?

1188
00:51:26,930 --> 00:51:29,030
The effect of a
drug simulated might

1189
00:51:29,030 --> 00:51:31,820
look like modifying the
spatial transcriptomics

1190
00:51:31,820 --> 00:51:35,850
data where you say this drug
suppresses this protein.

1191
00:51:35,850 --> 00:51:37,580
And So you could say
that the way that

1192
00:51:37,580 --> 00:51:39,830
would look in a spatial
transcriptomics data

1193
00:51:39,830 --> 00:51:43,670
is suppressing the leads of one
gene or a combination of genes.

1194
00:51:43,670 --> 00:51:46,730
And you're going to run
this counterfactual.

1195
00:51:46,730 --> 00:51:50,400
A what-if of, well, what
if I had applied that drug,

1196
00:51:50,400 --> 00:51:52,160
changed the spatial
transcriptomics

1197
00:51:52,160 --> 00:51:54,560
data in this way,
and run that forward

1198
00:51:54,560 --> 00:51:56,840
to a prediction of saying,
well, how does that change

1199
00:51:56,840 --> 00:51:59,900
the output of the model
trying to predict the protein

1200
00:51:59,900 --> 00:52:01,350
image on the other side?

1201
00:52:01,350 --> 00:52:04,740
And I have a small animation
showing how that might happen.

1202
00:52:04,740 --> 00:52:08,040
So this is a sample
from a patient.

1203
00:52:08,040 --> 00:52:09,540
The yellow here
are immune cells.

1204
00:52:09,540 --> 00:52:11,810
The pink is tumor.

1205
00:52:11,810 --> 00:52:14,900
Now, you can run two what-ifs.

1206
00:52:14,900 --> 00:52:17,750
In one what-if
simulation, you're

1207
00:52:17,750 --> 00:52:21,020
cranking up the expression
of this gene whose identity

1208
00:52:21,020 --> 00:52:22,050
is redacted here.

1209
00:52:22,050 --> 00:52:24,080
And in the other one,
you're suppressing

1210
00:52:24,080 --> 00:52:25,290
the activity of that gene.

1211
00:52:25,290 --> 00:52:27,530
So now I'm back to
creating synthetic data

1212
00:52:27,530 --> 00:52:29,520
on my multimodal input arms.

1213
00:52:29,520 --> 00:52:33,560
And I'm going to now run both
of those parallel universes

1214
00:52:33,560 --> 00:52:34,530
forward.

1215
00:52:34,530 --> 00:52:38,780
And what I'm looking
for is the readout

1216
00:52:38,780 --> 00:52:41,490
is one of these 16 channels
that's being predicted.

1217
00:52:41,490 --> 00:52:44,690
In this case, I'm looking for
a tumor immunogenicity marker

1218
00:52:44,690 --> 00:52:47,460
where higher would
indicate more favorable.

1219
00:52:47,460 --> 00:52:50,450
And you can see that this
simulation is telling us,

1220
00:52:50,450 --> 00:52:53,670
yeah, you should crank up the
expression of gene 1 if you can,

1221
00:52:53,670 --> 00:52:55,830
because it leads to a
more favorable outcome

1222
00:52:55,830 --> 00:52:56,910
on the other side.

1223
00:52:56,910 --> 00:53:00,320
So one way that we often
are using multimodal models

1224
00:53:00,320 --> 00:53:04,280
is to say we want this backbone
that makes predictions to tell

1225
00:53:04,280 --> 00:53:09,260
us what would change if we could
impart a delta or a modification

1226
00:53:09,260 --> 00:53:13,400
or a perturbation on another
one of the input streams.

1227
00:53:13,400 --> 00:53:18,140
So how do you go
[INAUDIBLE] there?

1228
00:53:18,140 --> 00:53:21,800
Well, if you're able to express
your hypothesis for what

1229
00:53:21,800 --> 00:53:24,900
a drug should do in the
input space of the model,

