and then we have biologists
and immunologists in-house.

1472
01:03:47,800 --> 01:03:50,470
I didn't talk about this at all,
but we have an in vivo platform

1473
01:03:50,470 --> 01:03:54,500
with mice where you can actually
go test hypotheses about saying,

1474
01:03:54,500 --> 01:03:56,750
here's a mouse model for
this patient population.

1475
01:03:56,750 --> 01:03:58,640
We think that this
drug will be effective.

1476
01:03:58,640 --> 01:03:59,720
Let's go test it.

1477
01:03:59,720 --> 01:04:03,580
So we, right now, actually
do not provide any of these.

1478
01:04:03,580 --> 01:04:06,140
Even if you wanted to pay me
today to go use one of these,

1479
01:04:06,140 --> 01:04:07,790
I don't have an API
endpoint for you,

1480
01:04:07,790 --> 01:04:09,790
but we are using
these internally

1481
01:04:09,790 --> 01:04:12,830
to do drug discovery and
basic discovery every day.

1482
01:04:12,830 --> 01:04:15,530
So the model is like a
lower latency version,

1483
01:04:15,530 --> 01:04:17,480
and then the mouse is just to--

1484
01:04:17,480 --> 01:04:21,040
so you do most of your
testing with the model,

1485
01:04:21,040 --> 01:04:23,680
I assume, and then the
mouse is just a few--

1486
01:04:23,680 --> 01:04:27,370
after you do the initial
filtering with the AI model,

1487
01:04:27,370 --> 01:04:28,370
or how does that work?

1488
01:04:28,370 --> 01:04:31,010
Yeah, so the model can
tell you two things.

1489
01:04:31,010 --> 01:04:32,510
In the best case,
it tells you, hey,

1490
01:04:32,510 --> 01:04:35,140
you should go try
this drug in mice.

1491
01:04:35,140 --> 01:04:37,210
We don't pretend
that this model is

1492
01:04:37,210 --> 01:04:40,240
going to understand all of the
complex biology that happens

1493
01:04:40,240 --> 01:04:41,930
when you administer a drug.

1494
01:04:41,930 --> 01:04:45,200
So you still need to go to
that in vivo test case and say,

1495
01:04:45,200 --> 01:04:47,290
is this actually working
to get rid of tumor?

1496
01:04:47,290 --> 01:04:49,960
The other thing the models tell
us is maybe there's already

1497
01:04:49,960 --> 01:04:52,120
a drug people have tried,
but they're trying it

1498
01:04:52,120 --> 01:04:53,900
in the wrong population.

1499
01:04:53,900 --> 01:04:56,290
They're enrolling too
broad of a clinical trial,

1500
01:04:56,290 --> 01:04:58,190
because they don't
know that actually,

1501
01:04:58,190 --> 01:05:00,610
this small sliver
of the patient space

1502
01:05:00,610 --> 01:05:03,230
is the one that's going to
respond well to your drug.

1503
01:05:03,230 --> 01:05:05,830
And So we are using the
model in that capacity

1504
01:05:05,830 --> 01:05:09,760
to give us ideas for things
to try in our in vivo platform

1505
01:05:09,760 --> 01:05:13,420
or to re-examine clinical data
and see if we can understand why

1506
01:05:13,420 --> 01:05:15,490
clinical trials have
failed, where other people

1507
01:05:15,490 --> 01:05:17,695
are kind of shrugging and
going, well, I don't know,

1508
01:05:17,695 --> 01:05:20,710
10 people were totally
cured, but the other 190

1509
01:05:20,710 --> 01:05:22,330
weren't, and we don't know why.

1510
01:05:22,330 --> 01:05:23,060
That makes sense.

1511
01:05:23,060 --> 01:05:28,120
So is the end game to completely
replace clinical trials or--

1512
01:05:28,120 --> 01:05:29,060
I don't know.

1513
01:05:29,060 --> 01:05:31,670
I mean, maybe in
many, many years.

1514
01:05:31,670 --> 01:05:34,840
I think ultimately
safety and efficacy

1515
01:05:34,840 --> 01:05:37,753
proven out in humans will
need to happen at some point.

1516
01:05:37,753 --> 01:05:40,420
If anything, I would assume that
the earlier part of that funnel

1517
01:05:40,420 --> 01:05:43,250
is going to be increasingly
done synthetically,

1518
01:05:43,250 --> 01:05:46,240
that maybe we say you
can, in some cases,

1519
01:05:46,240 --> 01:05:49,385
skip a mouse model if you have
a very clear mechanism of action

1520
01:05:49,385 --> 01:05:51,010
and you want to go
straight to checking

1521
01:05:51,010 --> 01:05:52,700
for safety and efficacy.

1522
01:05:52,700 --> 01:05:53,200
Thanks.

1523
01:05:53,200 --> 01:05:55,660
Yeah, thank you.

1524
01:05:55,660 --> 01:05:57,040
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

1525
01:05:57,040 --> 01:05:59,860
--scenarios of
simulations to test

1526
01:05:59,860 --> 01:06:01,280
with many different
combinations.

1527
01:06:01,280 --> 01:06:04,400
I'm wondering how you
narrow it down to prioritize

1528
01:06:04,400 --> 01:06:06,080
which one to test first.

1529
01:06:06,080 --> 01:06:07,850
Yeah, that's a
fantastic question.

1530
01:06:07,850 --> 01:06:09,710
So I think of what
we're doing is

1531
01:06:09,710 --> 01:06:13,610
like, let's say you had a really
good world model, a simulator

1532
01:06:13,610 --> 01:06:14,880
of the world.

1533
01:06:14,880 --> 01:06:17,240
Even in the macroscopic
case, you still

1534
01:06:17,240 --> 01:06:20,190
need an agent to tell you
what is worth simulating.

1535
01:06:20,190 --> 01:06:21,900
You can't just try
things at random.

1536
01:06:21,900 --> 01:06:25,530
And I think there's two
promising things here.

1537
01:06:25,530 --> 01:06:28,370
One of them is you still have
subject matter experts that

1538
01:06:28,370 --> 01:06:30,710
say, oh, great, you've
given me a perfect simulator

1539
01:06:30,710 --> 01:06:31,760
of patient biology.

1540
01:06:31,760 --> 01:06:33,260
I know the first
30 things I'm going

1541
01:06:33,260 --> 01:06:36,500
to try because I'm familiar
with the things that have

1542
01:06:36,500 --> 01:06:37,860
been considered in this space.

1543
01:06:37,860 --> 01:06:40,520
We have hypotheses for
how the tumor is evading

1544
01:06:40,520 --> 01:06:42,020
the immune system,
and we want to go

1545
01:06:42,020 --> 01:06:43,530
see if we can disrupt those.

1546
01:06:43,530 --> 01:06:46,432
The other thing
that I don't know

1547
01:06:46,432 --> 01:06:48,390
how much weight I want
to put behind right now,

1548
01:06:48,390 --> 01:06:51,000
but would be a little
foolish to dismiss,

1549
01:06:51,000 --> 01:06:55,190
is that we have these LLMs now
that are trained on basically

1550
01:06:55,190 --> 01:06:57,210
the entirety of
scientific literature,

1551
01:06:57,210 --> 01:06:59,160
know about a bunch of studies.

1552
01:06:59,160 --> 01:07:02,480
And if they have access
to effectively tool use

1553
01:07:02,480 --> 01:07:05,210
and say, OK, what experiments
do you want to run?

1554
01:07:05,210 --> 01:07:09,280
And start thinking about virtual
scientists or AI scientist

1555
01:07:09,280 --> 01:07:11,380
agents that are
trying to decide,

1556
01:07:11,380 --> 01:07:13,280
this is the next
experiment I would do.

1557
01:07:13,280 --> 01:07:14,740
And maybe at some point,
you close the loop

1558
01:07:14,740 --> 01:07:16,390
and you say, great, we're
going to go run that experiment

1559
01:07:16,390 --> 01:07:18,310
and we'll be back to you
tomorrow and then tell me

1560
01:07:18,310 --> 01:07:19,220
the next thing to try.

1561
01:07:19,220 --> 01:07:20,595
But either way,
you need somebody

1562
01:07:20,595 --> 01:07:22,540
who's familiar with the
domain, whether that's

1563
01:07:22,540 --> 01:07:25,030
an LLM or a scientist,
to make the decisions

1564
01:07:25,030 --> 01:07:27,330
about how to use the simulator.

1565
01:07:27,330 --> 01:07:28,300
Got it, thanks.

1566
01:07:28,300 --> 01:07:29,120
Yeah, thank you.

1567
01:07:29,120 --> 01:07:31,720

1568
01:07:31,720 --> 01:07:36,680
Yeah, so I know your grand
vision is to cure cancer.

1569
01:07:36,680 --> 01:07:42,040
Do you have some thoughts on
cancer prevention, probably

1570
01:07:42,040 --> 01:07:43,640
vaccines and stuff like that?

1571
01:07:43,640 --> 01:07:45,710
Yeah, that is a good question.

1572
01:07:45,710 --> 01:07:49,030
I'm not sure I have a fantastic
answer about how are we thinking

1573
01:07:49,030 --> 01:07:50,180
about cancer prevention.

1574
01:07:50,180 --> 01:07:51,940
It relates to this
problem of, we

1575
01:07:51,940 --> 01:07:53,990
don't have healthy
data to look at.

1576
01:07:53,990 --> 01:07:57,490
And if there isn't an indicator
right now in the clinic of,

1577
01:07:57,490 --> 01:07:59,800
yeah, you look healthy
today, but we're still

1578
01:07:59,800 --> 01:08:03,040
going to go biopsy this
and send it off and fit you

1579
01:08:03,040 --> 01:08:04,310
into this framework.

1580
01:08:04,310 --> 01:08:06,140
Then I think you're
going to be limited

1581
01:08:06,140 --> 01:08:07,940
in what you can do
about simulating

1582
01:08:07,940 --> 01:08:11,540
the patient biology of patients
that, right now, are totally

1583
01:08:11,540 --> 01:08:12,630
asymptomatic.

1584
01:08:12,630 --> 01:08:14,010
I could imagine that changing.

1585
01:08:14,010 --> 01:08:16,007
I mean, it's possible
that data acquisition

1586
01:08:16,007 --> 01:08:17,840
is going to be broad
enough and cheap enough

1587
01:08:17,840 --> 01:08:21,569
that it is just routine to
have a little bit of H&E taken.

1588
01:08:21,569 --> 01:08:24,140
And maybe there is
information in the latent

1589
01:08:24,140 --> 01:08:26,157
space of this model
that says, yeah,

1590
01:08:26,157 --> 01:08:27,740
this looks like it's
over in this part

1591
01:08:27,740 --> 01:08:29,160
of the patient distribution.

1592
01:08:29,160 --> 01:08:34,069
Maybe we keep an eye on this
patient over the next few years.

1593
01:08:34,069 --> 01:08:35,779
Same question.

1594
01:08:35,779 --> 01:08:36,470
Great.

1595
01:08:36,470 --> 01:08:38,060
I have a follow-up question.

1596
01:08:38,060 --> 01:08:40,740
Google scientist, AlphaFold,
and all this stuff.

1597
01:08:40,740 --> 01:08:44,090
Yesterday, Microsoft released
a co-pilot or semi autopilot

1598
01:08:44,090 --> 01:08:46,310
for science discovery tool.

1599
01:08:46,310 --> 01:08:51,649
I'm very curious how we can
run a billion experiments

1600
01:08:51,649 --> 01:08:52,830
autonomously.

1601
01:08:52,830 --> 01:08:58,670
Maybe instead of modeling
a world scenario, maybe

1602
01:08:58,670 --> 01:09:03,830
model a person as an agent
and figure out what drugs

1603
01:09:03,830 --> 01:09:06,920
could potentially cure the--

1604
01:09:06,920 --> 01:09:10,439
context, we just lost someone
like two weeks ago to cancer.

1605
01:09:10,439 --> 01:09:12,200
So this is a very
big thing for me.

1606
01:09:12,200 --> 01:09:18,000
How do we prevent or cure
in the intermediate cancer?

1607
01:09:18,000 --> 01:09:19,939
So that's the way I'm
thinking about where

1608
01:09:19,939 --> 01:09:23,540
agents and science intersect.

1609
01:09:23,540 --> 01:09:25,842
Yeah, I have a
few thoughts here.

1610
01:09:25,842 --> 01:09:27,300
I'm not sure how
coherent they are.

1611
01:09:27,300 --> 01:09:32,569
One of them is I am not sure
yet that speed and scale is

1612
01:09:32,569 --> 01:09:34,740
the bottleneck on running
these simulations.

1613
01:09:34,740 --> 01:09:37,880
I have not also
seen an example yet

1614
01:09:37,880 --> 01:09:40,580
of an agent come up with a
really brilliant experiment

1615
01:09:40,580 --> 01:09:42,229
that our scientists
didn't write down

1616
01:09:42,229 --> 01:09:43,819
in the first four
minutes of thinking

1617
01:09:43,819 --> 01:09:45,300
about how to use this thing.

1618
01:09:45,300 --> 01:09:47,310
That doesn't mean I'm
not optimistic about it.

1619
01:09:47,310 --> 01:09:49,260
I think these systems
are going to get better.

1620
01:09:49,260 --> 01:09:52,642
And if anything, I have been
wrong every time on saying like,

1621
01:09:52,642 --> 01:09:53,850
yeah, that's five years away.

1622
01:09:53,850 --> 01:09:56,267
And then five months later it
turns out that we can do it.

1623
01:09:56,267 --> 01:09:59,060
So I would like to
remain optimistic

1624
01:09:59,060 --> 01:10:01,980
that we're going to be able
to frame these problems,

1625
01:10:01,980 --> 01:10:03,740
integrate things like
tool use in a way

1626
01:10:03,740 --> 01:10:07,290
that does allow agents to go
run a bunch of experiments.

1627
01:10:07,290 --> 01:10:09,950
And then for us, we probably
still go test those out in mice

1628
01:10:09,950 --> 01:10:12,450
and then think about
them in that capacity.

1629
01:10:12,450 --> 01:10:14,540
But yeah, it's a very
interesting direction

1630
01:10:14,540 --> 01:10:16,634
to think about.

1631
01:10:16,634 --> 01:10:17,960
[APPLAUSE]

1632
01:10:17,960 --> 01:10:24,350
OK, give another hand to Misha
for the very interesting talk.

1633
01:10:24,350 --> 01:10:27,710
Yeah, so you'll notice
this talk was different

1634
01:10:27,710 --> 01:10:30,560
from a lot of the more
recent talks or work,

1635
01:10:30,560 --> 01:10:32,250
just focusing more on LLMs.

1636
01:10:32,250 --> 01:10:37,610
But I think this is a very
important space and an area,

1637
01:10:37,610 --> 01:10:40,520
application area, where
machine learning and AI can

1638
01:10:40,520 --> 01:10:44,520
make a lot of impact and save
the world, change the world,

1639
01:10:44,520 --> 01:10:47,210
positively impact the
world and save lives.

1640
01:10:47,210 --> 01:10:49,310
So we should
definitely encourage

1641
01:10:49,310 --> 01:10:51,890
more work for machine
learning and AI

1642
01:10:51,890 --> 01:10:57,700
for this area of cancer research
and healthcare in general.

1643
01:10:57,700 --> 01:11:02,000

